window.KB_EXPLAIN_EN_ADD({"L1-1.1.1":"Nearly all mainstream modern LLMs are decoder-only Transformers. In 2023–2025 a \"standard recipe\" emerged, typified by Llama / Qwen / Mistral: Pre-RMSNorm + RoPE + SwiGLU + GQA. It solves the problem of autoregressive generation with a single, indefinitely scalable architecture.\n\n**How it works**\n- A BPE tokenizer turns text into token ids, which are looked up in the embedding table `E ∈ R^{V×d}` to get `x_0` — the start of the residual stream.\n- Each block: `h = x + Attn(RMSNorm(x))`, where attention applies RoPE to q and k, uses a causal mask to see only the left context, and uses GQA so multiple query heads share K/V; then `x = h + FFN(RMSNorm(h))`, with a SwiGLU FFN.\n- After L layers, a final RMSNorm and the LM head (`W_U ∈ R^{d×V}`) produce logits; take the last position, sample the next token according to the decoding strategy, append it and repeat, with already-computed K/V stored in the KV cache.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nEach component maps to an engineering motivation: Pre-LN keeps deep stacks stable, RoPE provides relative position and is KV-cache friendly, SwiGLU does better at the same parameter count, and GQA shrinks inference memory. In 2026 attention layers are starting to become local / sparse / linear hybrids, but the skeleton is still this recipe.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- When asked to \"explain the Transformer\", state decoder-only + the four components in one sentence, then ask which part they want to go deeper on.\n- Be able to draw the data flow of a forward pass and say at which step the KV cache is produced.","L1-1.1.2":"The encoder-decoder Transformer from 2017's \"Attention Is All You Need\" and today's decoder-only LLMs differ in almost every component. This comparison question tests whether you know not just what changed but why.\n\n**How it works** (item-by-item comparison)\n- Overall architecture: encoder-decoder → decoder-only, for a unified objective, easy scaling and KV-cache friendliness.\n- Positional encoding: sinusoidal, added to the input → RoPE, multiplied into q/k in every layer, giving relative position and better extrapolation.\n- Normalization: Post-LN → Pre-LN for stable deep training; LayerNorm → RMSNorm (often with QK-Norm), cheaper with comparable results.\n- FFN: ReLU with 4d hidden → SwiGLU with about 8d/3 hidden, better at the same parameter count.\n- Attention heads: MHA → GQA or MLA, shrinking the KV cache; implementation: naive → FlashAttention, which never materializes the N×N matrix.\n- FFN structure: dense → MoE is now common in large models, decoupling parameter count from compute.\n- Biases / dropout: mostly removed — unnecessary at large data scale, and simpler for quantization.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nMost of these changes serve two goals: stability of large-scale training, and memory and bandwidth costs at inference. Understanding the motivations handles follow-ups better than memorizing the table.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Be able to name at least 6 changes and the reason for each.\n- When asked \"which change mattered most?\", answer along two axes: training stability (Pre-LN) and inference cost (GQA, FlashAttention).","L1-1.1.3":"Llama and Qwen are the two main open-source LLM families. Comparing them tests whether you can use config-level details to explain \"different trade-offs on the same skeleton\".\n\n**How it works**\n- What they share: decoder-only, Pre-RMSNorm, RoPE, GQA, SwiGLU; large models don't tie embeddings.\n- Differences (per the open-source configs): Llama-3-8B has 32 layers, d_ff 14336, a ~128k vocabulary and RoPE base 500,000; Qwen3-8B has 36 layers, d_ff 12288, a ~150k vocabulary and RoPE base 1,000,000, compressing Chinese and multilingual text better.\n- Stability details: Qwen2.5's QKV projections have bias; Qwen3 drops the bias and adds QK-Norm. Qwen3 configures head_dim independently: the 0.6B model has 16 heads × 128 = 2048, larger than its d_model of 1024.\n- MoE: Qwen3 routes to 8 of 128 experts, with no shared experts and a global-batch load-balancing loss.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nReading the config directly lets you compute the KV cache: in BF16, Llama-3-8B uses 128 KiB per token, while Qwen2.5-7B, with only 4 KV heads, uses 56 KiB; Qwen3-0.6B's KV at 32k context is about 3.5 GiB, three times its weights. In 2026 Qwen3.5 / 3.8 moved to a 3:1 hybrid of Gated DeltaNet and attention.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Answer order: shared skeleton → two or three differences with numbers → one line on the latest evolution.\n- \"Why did Qwen3 add QK-Norm?\": constraining the attention-logit scale buys stability — a common inference.","L1-1.1.4":"Transformers fall into three paradigms by how they use attention — encoder-only, encoder-decoder and decoder-only — corresponding to understanding, transduction and generation tasks respectively.\n\n**How it works**\n- Encoder-only: bidirectional self-attention, pretrained with MLM (mask about 15% of tokens and predict them); good at classification, NER, retrieval and embeddings; representatives: BERT, RoBERTa, DeBERTa, ModernBERT.\n- Encoder-decoder: the encoder encodes the input bidirectionally, and the decoder generates causally while reading the encoder output via cross-attention; objectives are mostly span corruption / denoising; good at tasks where input and output structures differ a lot, such as translation, summarization and speech recognition; representatives: the original Transformer, T5, BART, Whisper.\n- Decoder-only: causal self-attention only, with next-token prediction as the objective; good at generation and anything that can be framed as continuation; representatives: the GPT series, Llama, Qwen, Claude, Gemini.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nBidirectional attention sees the full context at once and is more efficient for understanding tasks; causal attention naturally fits token-by-token generation and supports the KV cache. Choose based on task shape and cost, not which one is \"more advanced\".\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Be able to explain the differences along four columns: attention form, pretraining objective, strengths, representative models.\n- Common follow-ups: why decoder-only became mainstream, and whether encoders are still useful.","L1-1.1.5":"Decoder-only models became the LLM mainstream not because bidirectional attention is bad, but because decoder-only is the best fit for scaling on three levels: objective, usage pattern and engineering.\n\n**How it works**\n- The most general objective with the densest signal: next-token prediction needs no labels or paired data, so any text can be used; a sequence of length T provides T supervision signals, whereas MLM puts loss on only about 15% of positions.\n- Pretraining matches usage: training is continuation and so is inference, every task can be written as prompt → completion, and in-context learning emerges naturally; MLM has `[MASK]` during training but not at inference, a mismatch.\n- The simplest engineering: a single stack, a single block type, no cross-attention; the causal mask makes the KV cache and prefix caching possible, so multi-turn conversations only compute new tokens, whereas an encoder-decoder must re-encode whenever the input changes.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nIn a paradigm where data scale sets the ceiling, the objective that can absorb all the data wins. The cost is that decoders aren't necessarily the most economical choice for narrow understanding tasks.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Structure the answer as objective → data → engineering → capabilities, rather than just saying \"it's better at generation\".\n- Closing line: the next-token objective scales best and the causal structure makes caching and streaming generation simple; for narrow understanding tasks, encoders are still more economical.","L1-1.1.6":"These are supplementary arguments to \"why decoder-only won\": parameter allocation efficiency, the uselessness of bidirectional information during generation, and the ecosystem's path dependence.\n\n**How it works**\n- Parameter allocation: an encoder-decoder splits parameters across two stacks; in long-output, multi-turn settings, decoder-only devotes all parameters to modeling a single sequence, using them more efficiently.\n- The extra information from bidirectional attention goes unused: during generation, future tokens don't exist yet, so bidirectionality has nothing to do. There's also a more theoretical hypothesis: a causal attention matrix is lower triangular with a positive diagonal and is therefore full rank, whereas a bidirectional attention matrix can degenerate to low rank, actually limiting expressiveness.\n- Path dependence: after GPT-3, compute investment, data pipelines and the toolchain — inference engines, quantization, fine-tuning libraries — were all optimized around decoder-only, creating a positive feedback loop.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nThese points show the win has both technical and ecosystem causes. When answering, flag which claims are consensus and which are hypotheses (such as the full-rank argument) to show rigor.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- The \"full rank\" argument is only supplementary; say clearly that it's a hypothesis.\n- Path dependence means even a better architecture needs the toolchain to catch up before migration — one reason hybrid architectures still keep the Transformer skeleton.","L1-1.1.7":"Encoders didn't disappear when decoder-only became mainstream: for narrow understanding tasks such as retrieval embeddings, classification and intent detection, small bidirectional encoders are often still the most cost-effective choice.\n\n**How it works**\n- A bidirectional encoder with a few hundred million parameters is often both faster and better than a multi-billion-parameter decoder at retrieval embeddings, classification and entity extraction; a lot of production intent classification, safety filtering and reranking runs on such models.\n- ModernBERT (December 2024) brought modern components such as RoPE, alternating local / global attention and FlashAttention back to encoders, with native support for 8192 tokens.\n- Encoder-decoders still have an edge in speech recognition (Whisper) and some translation and summarization; in 2025 Google also released work converting Gemma into an encoder-decoder.\n- LLMs have \"encoder-like\" uses too: when fine-tuned into embedding models, the causal mask is often removed or the last token's representation is used.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nBidirectional attention uses left and right context together and is more efficient for \"understanding\"; when the output space is small there's no need for generation, and a small model cuts latency and cost significantly.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- When asked \"are encoders obsolete?\", don't be absolute; cite ModernBERT and production classification / reranking.\n- Tie it to architecture design: a small encoder handles high-frequency intents and escalates low-confidence cases to an LLM.","L1-1.1.8":"When an LLM misclassifies user intent, treat it as a falsifiable diagnostic problem: form hypotheses per module and distinguish them with controlled experiments, rather than tweaking the prompt by feel.\n\n**How it works**\n- Hypotheses by module: tokenizer (model numbers, abbreviations and mixed Chinese-English text get fragmented) → position and long context (an early intent gets ignored) → attention (pulled off course by distractors or earlier topics) → FFN / knowledge (unfamiliar domain terms) → post-training and templates (sycophantic phrasing, inconsistent templates) → decoding (different results across runs).\n- Investigation order: first slice by language, length, turn count and domain to find where errors concentrate → minimal reproduction → perturbation experiments (shorten the context, reorder, rephrase, remove distractors) to see whether the prediction flips → take the logprob of each intent label's first token and look at the margin between first and second place → only then touch the model.\n- Targeted fixes: for context problems, compress history and rewrite the intent; for missing knowledge, use a lexicon / RAG / fine-tuning; for template problems, fix the template; for unstable decoding, use low temperature, constrained decoding or voting.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nThis kind of question tests engineering methodology. Attention heatmaps are only clues — weights are not explanations — and need to be verified with occlusion / ablation.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Error rate rising monotonically with the length of preceding distractors → long-context / attention dilution; still wrong in the simplest context → missing knowledge.\n- Few-shot order sensitivity: run several random orderings, report mean and variance, and calibrate.","L1-1.2.1":"Subword tokenization is a compromise between \"word level\" and \"character / byte level\": with a fixed-size vocabulary, frequent words are stored whole and rare words are split into pieces, so in principle there are no OOV words.\n\n**How it works**\n- Word level: a huge, open vocabulary and short sequences, but with OOV, morphological variation and vocabulary explosion across languages.\n- Character / byte level: a tiny vocabulary (256 or a few thousand) but very long sequences, high O(n²) attention cost, and weak semantics per unit.\n- Subword: vocabularies of 30k–260k, with moderate sequence lengths. It is essentially data-driven compression — encoding the corpus in as few tokens as possible. The main algorithms are BPE, WordPiece and Unigram.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nTokenization directly determines three engineering quantities: how much fits in the context, how much each call costs, and how long it takes. A larger vocabulary means shorter sequences but more embedding / LM head parameters and less training for rare tokens. Compression rates also differ by language, affecting the cost and fairness of multilingual products.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Explain the problems with each of the three granularities and why subword is the compromise.\n- Connect the tokenizer to cost, context capacity and latency to show an engineering perspective.","L1-1.2.2":"BPE (Byte Pair Encoding) is the tokenization algorithm used by the vast majority of generative models — the GPT series, Llama, Qwen, DeepSeek and others. The idea is to repeatedly merge the most frequent adjacent pair, bottom up.\n\n**How it works**\n- Training: initial vocabulary = all base units (characters or 256 bytes) → count the frequency of every adjacent pair in the corpus → merge the most frequent pair into a new token, add it to the vocabulary and record the merge rule → repeat until the vocabulary reaches the target size.\n- Encoding: split text into base units and apply the merge rules in the order they were learned; the result is deterministic.\n- A minimal implementation just uses `Counter(zip(ids, ids[1:]))` to find the most frequent pair, replaces that pair in the sequence with a new id, and loops num_merges times.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nBPE is simple, deterministic and easy to implement, and with byte-level base units it eliminates `<unk>` entirely. Compared with WordPiece, BPE merges by frequency rather than likelihood gain; compared with Unigram, BPE merges bottom up while Unigram prunes top down. Real systems also apply a regex pre-split first to prevent merges across words.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Be able to hand-code the training loop, and explain that \"encoding applies merges in learned order\" rather than re-finding the most frequent pair each time.\n- Differences from WordPiece and Unigram: direction, merge / retention criterion, encoding method.","L1-1.2.3":"WordPiece and the Unigram Language Model are the two mainstream subword algorithms besides BPE: WordPiece is used by the BERT family, and Unigram is SentencePiece's default algorithm, used by T5, ALBERT and XLNet.\n\n**How it works**\n- WordPiece: also merges bottom up, but the criterion isn't highest frequency; it's the pair that most increases language-model likelihood, often written `score(a,b) = count(ab) / (count(a) × count(b))`, favoring pairs that \"occur together far more often than independently\". Encoding uses greedy longest match (find the longest vocabulary prefix from left to right), and word-internal pieces carry a `##` prefix, e.g. `play ##ing`.\n- Unigram: the opposite approach, top down. Build a very large candidate vocabulary, assume each subword occurs independently, estimate probabilities with EM, and repeatedly prune the subwords that contribute least to total likelihood until reaching the target size. Encoding uses Viterbi to find the most probable segmentation; you can also sample different segmentations by probability (subword regularization), which acts as data augmentation.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nComparison: BPE merges by frequency and encodes deterministically in merge order; WordPiece merges by likelihood gain and uses greedy longest match; Unigram prunes by the likelihood loss from removal and encodes with Viterbi, optionally sampling. Unigram's probabilistic model naturally supports multiple segmentations, which makes it good for regularization.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Compare along four dimensions: direction, merge / retention criterion, encoding method, typical models.\n- \"Which is better, SentencePiece or BPE?\" is a false question; compare BPE with Unigram instead.","L1-1.2.4":"Byte-level BPE swaps BPE's base units from Unicode characters to UTF-8 bytes (256 of them), eliminating the out-of-vocabulary problem at the root.\n\n**How it works**\n- The initial vocabulary is fixed at 256 bytes; any string can be converted to a byte sequence and then merged according to the merge rules, so there is never an `<unk>`.\n- The cost is byte overhead for non-Latin scripts: a Chinese character takes 3 bytes in UTF-8, so if it isn't merged into a larger token it takes 3 tokens. Frequent Chinese characters usually get merged during training, while rare characters and emoji are often split into multiple byte tokens.\n- GPT-2 was the first to adopt it at scale (vocabulary 50,257); the tiktoken encodings and tokenizers from Llama 3 onward are also byte-level BPE.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nPros: 100% coverage, a uniform implementation, no need to prepare character sets for each language. Cons: poor compression for languages underrepresented in the training corpus, so the same meaning needs more tokens, hurting context capacity and cost. The larger the vocabulary and the richer the multilingual corpus, the smaller this disadvantage.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Explain why byte-level has no OOV and why a Chinese character may take 3 tokens.\n- Distinguish it from SentencePiece's byte fallback, which is primarily character-level and only falls back to bytes for unknown characters.","L1-1.2.5":"SuperBPE is a BPE improvement proposed in March 2025: it splits training into two stages, first learning subwords within whitespace boundaries and then lifting pre-tokenization to learn \"superwords\" that span whitespace, improving compression.\n\n**How it works**\n- Standard BPE pre-splits on whitespace and punctuation with a regex before training to prevent cross-word merges, so each token is at most one word.\n- In SuperBPE's first stage, pre-tokenization is kept and subwords are learned normally; in the second stage, the whitespace constraint is removed and merging continues, learning multi-word tokens that span spaces (such as common phrases). It's a curriculum-style \"constrain first, then relax\".\n- Reported results: with a 200k vocabulary, the token count for the same text drops by up to 33%, with an average improvement of 4.0% across 30 downstream tasks.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nToken count directly determines context capacity, inference cost and latency, so better compression means processing more text for the same compute. The cost is that the cross-word merges pre-tokenization was meant to prevent are now allowed, so merge quality has to be controlled in stages. Chinese has no whitespace to begin with, so it benefits differently from English.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- If asked \"why is pre-tokenization necessary / what does it cost?\": it prevents cross-word merges but also caps compression; SuperBPE compromises by relaxing it in stages.\n- Remedies for poor Chinese token efficiency: expand the vocabulary, train merges on Chinese corpora, or consider cross-whitespace merging methods.","L1-1.2.6":"SentencePiece and tiktoken are often conflated with BPE, but both are implementations, not algorithms: the former is a library implementing BPE and Unigram, the latter OpenAI's high-performance BPE implementation.\n\n**How it works**\n- SentencePiece: treats input as a raw character stream without relying on pre-tokenization, explicitly encodes spaces as `▁`, and is losslessly reversible from encoding to decoding, which suits languages without spaces such as Chinese and Japanese. It can run BPE or Unigram; used by Llama 1/2 (32k-vocabulary BPE), T5 and Gemma.\n- tiktoken: a byte-level BPE implementation with a Rust core. Common encodings: `cl100k_base` (GPT-3.5/4, vocabulary 100,257), `o200k_base` (GPT-4o, the o series, the GPT-5 series, about 200k), `o200k_harmony` (gpt-oss, adding special tokens for the harmony chat format on top of o200k, 201,088 in total). From Llama 3 onward, Llama also switched to tiktoken-style BPE with a vocabulary of about 128k.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nThe choice of library affects speed, reversibility and how special tokens are handled: by default tiktoken raises an error when it meets special-token text, requiring an explicit `allowed_special`, which matters for preventing token-level injection.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- \"Which is better, SentencePiece or BPE?\" is a false question: one is a library, the other an algorithm; the proper comparisons are BPE vs Unigram, or the tiktoken vs SentencePiece implementations.\n- Be able to name 2–3 tiktoken encodings and their models.","L1-1.2.7":"SentencePiece's key design choice is to encode spaces as an ordinary symbol `▁` (U+2581), so it doesn't depend on pre-tokenization and guarantees lossless round-tripping between encoding and decoding.\n\n**How it works**\n- Traditional tokenization first splits on spaces and then applies subwords within words; space information is lost at splitting time, and decoding can only guess by rule where spaces belong.\n- SentencePiece treats the whole input as a raw character stream, first replaces spaces with `▁`, then trains BPE or Unigram on that stream. Spaces thus become part of tokens, e.g. `▁hello` means \"hello preceded by a space\".\n- To decode, concatenate all tokens and turn `▁` back into spaces, reconstructing the original text exactly with no language-specific post-processing.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nLossless reversibility lets the same pipeline work for languages without spaces such as Chinese and Japanese, and avoids the language bias of pre-tokenization rules. A side effect is that the same word with and without a leading space is a different token, so leading/trailing spaces in a prompt can change the continuation distribution.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Explain why SentencePiece is called language-agnostic: no reliance on pre-tokenization, and spaces are explicitly encoded.\n- Connect to engineering pitfalls: `\" hello\"` and `\"hello\"` are different tokens, so watch spaces when reading logits for few-shot classification.","L1-1.2.8":"Special tokens are vocabulary entries that don't correspond to natural text and mark structure; a chat template defines how a list of messages is assembled into a single token sequence. The model only ever saw one assembly format in training, and inference must match it.\n\n**How it works**\n- Three kinds of special tokens: sequence boundaries (`<s>` / `<|begin_of_text|>` as BOS, `</s>` / `<|endoftext|>` as EOS, `<pad>`); conversation structure (ChatML's `<|im_start|>`, `<|im_end|>`; Llama 3's `<|start_header_id|>`, `<|eot_id|>`); functional markers (`<tool_call>`, `<think>`, `<image>`).\n- Hugging Face stores the template as Jinja in `tokenizer_config.json` and renders it with `tok.apply_chat_template(messages, tokenize=False, add_generation_prompt=True)`; `add_generation_prompt` appends the assistant start marker at the end, signaling \"your turn\".\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nA wrong template usually doesn't raise an error; the model just \"gets dumber\": metrics drop, answers miss the point, generation won't stop. That makes it a hotspot for production incidents. Hand-concatenating strings is the most dangerous approach; always treat the official template's rendering as ground truth.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- How to debug: compare the output of `apply_chat_template` with the string actually being sent.\n- Be able to list common incidents: template mismatch, missing generation prompt, duplicate BOS, wrong EOS, special-token injection, template drift after fine-tuning.","L1-1.2.9":"Misconfigured chat templates are the most common \"silent\" incident when deploying open-source models: nothing errors, the model just performs worse or behaves strangely.\n\n**How it works** (five typical incidents)\n- Template mismatch: hand-built strings missing a newline or using the wrong markers; metrics drop, answers miss the point, generation never stops.\n- Forgetting `add_generation_prompt`: with no assistant start marker at the end, the model first continues writing as the user instead of answering.\n- Duplicate BOS: the template already includes BOS and the tokenizer adds another automatically — especially easy to hit during fine-tuning.\n- Wrong EOS / stop condition: when Llama 3 was first released, many inference stacks recognized only `<|end_of_text|>` and not `<|eot_id|>`, so generation wouldn't stop.\n- Special-token injection: user input containing text like `<|im_start|>system` gets encoded as real special tokens, effectively forging a system message; disable special-token parsing for user content.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nYou can quantify the impact with a \"breakage experiment\": compare accuracy, average output length and the share of runs that fail to stop under conditions such as the correct template, no generation prompt, another family's format, duplicate BOS and no stop token.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Write a template self-check function in under 20 lines: render messages and assert that the leading and trailing markers are as expected; reuse it for fine-tuning and deployment.\n- Special-token injection is prompt injection at the token level.","L1-1.2.10":"Template drift is when the chat template used during fine-tuning differs from the built-in template of the production inference framework (vLLM, Ollama, etc.), causing a clear drop in performance after deployment.\n\n**How it works**\n- During fine-tuning, training data is assembled into token sequences with a particular template, and what the model learns is \"what to say after these markers\".\n- At deployment, the inference framework may read the base model's own template, the framework's default template or a hand-written one; any difference in role markers, newlines, BOS/EOS or the position of the system section shifts the input distribution away from training.\n- Typical symptoms: excellent fine-tuning evals, but after launch the model answers off-topic, breaks formatting, won't stop or ignores system instructions.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nThese problems don't raise errors and are often misdiagnosed as \"fine-tuning didn't work\" or \"bad data\", making them costly to track down. The fundamental fix is to treat the template as part of the model's assets: training and inference share the template in the same `tokenizer_config.json`, with a consistency check before deployment.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Debugging: render the same messages on the training side and the inference side and compare token by token.\n- Prevention: after fine-tuning, ship the tokenizer and template together; specify the template explicitly at deployment instead of relying on framework defaults; add a template self-check unit test.","L1-1.2.11":"The tokenizer is not just a preprocessing detail: it directly affects cost estimates, model migration and several classic failure modes, and must be treated as a first-class citizen in engineering.\n\n**How it works**\n- Token counts vary by tokenizer: the same text can produce very different token counts on different models. Example: Anthropic changed tokenizers starting with Claude Opus 4.7; the official docs convert 1M tokens to about 555k English words, versus about 750k words before. The migration docs say the same text uses roughly 1× to 1.35× more tokens, and later models such as Opus 5.5 keep this tokenizer.\n- Numbers and character-level tasks: long numbers are split into irregular pieces (e.g. `1234567` → `123` `456` `7`), one reason early models were bad at arithmetic; letter-counting questions are hard because the model sees tokens, not letters.\n- Leading/trailing spaces: in BPE, `\" hello\"` and `\"hello\"` are different tokens, and an extra space at the end of a prompt can change the continuation distribution.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nWhen migrating models, re-measure token usage rather than only looking at the price list. A useful comparison experiment: take one passage each of English, Chinese, mixed text, Southeast Asian languages, code and JSON, and count tokens and characters per token under several tokenizers.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Give at least 3 counterintuitive splits: numbers, leading/trailing spaces, rare words.\n- State assumptions in cost estimates: text type, tokenizer, input/output ratio.","L1-1.2.12":"Tokens, words and characters are three different units of measure. When comparing prices or context lengths across models, first normalize to \"the actual number of tokens for the same text\".\n\n**How it works**\n- English rule of thumb: about 4 characters per token, about 0.75 words per token.\n- Chinese: common characters are usually about 1 token each; rare characters, mixed Chinese-English text and emoji take more. Under byte-level BPE, an unmerged Chinese character can take up to 3 tokens.\n- Tokenizers differ a lot: vocabulary size, the language mix of the training corpus and whether there's byte fallback all change the compression rate.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nAPIs charge per token and context windows are measured in tokens. Looking only at \"price per million tokens\", a model with poor compression looks cheap but may cost more to process the same text; the same goes for context windows — how much Chinese content fits in 128k tokens depends on the model.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- The right way to compare prices: take a batch of representative business text, encode it with each model's tokenizer, compute \"cost per thousand characters\" and compare.\n- Estimation questions commonly use the rules of thumb, but say that they are rules of thumb and depend on the tokenizer.","L1-1.2.13":"Under-trained tokens are tokens that exist in the vocabulary but appear extremely rarely in the model's training corpus. Their embeddings have barely been updated by gradients, and encountering them can trigger abnormal behavior.\n\n**How it works**\n- The tokenizer is usually trained separately on one corpus, while the model is trained on another (or a cleaned) corpus. If certain tokens are frequent in the tokenizer corpus but almost absent from the model corpus, they remain in the vocabulary without being adequately trained.\n- Their embeddings stay close to initialization and the model doesn't know how to handle them, which can lead to repetition, gibberish, refusals or ignoring instructions.\n- The larger the vocabulary, the more rare tokens there are, and the worse the problem.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nIt's part of the vocabulary-size trade-off: a large vocabulary shortens sequences but brings more parameters and more under-trained tokens. For safety and stability, such tokens can also be used to craft adversarial inputs.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Suspicious tokens can be detected using signals such as embedding norms and output-layer weights (a general approach).\n- Engineering mitigations: train the tokenizer and model on corpora with consistent distributions; normalize inputs; include robustness cases with rare tokens in evaluation.","L1-1.2.14":"Vocabulary size is a trade-off between sequence length and parameter count; and tokenizers trained mainly on English make other languages pay a higher token cost, creating a language-fairness problem.\n\n**How it works**\n- Large vocabulary: shorter sequences and more information per token, but more embedding and LM head parameters (V×d; e.g. with V=200k and d=4096, a single matrix is 820M parameters), and rare tokens are under-trained. A small vocabulary is the opposite. The recent trend is 100k–260k.\n- Language fairness: a tokenizer's merge rules come from its training corpus; when English dominates, Southeast Asian languages such as Thai, Vietnamese and Malay compress worse, needing more tokens for the same meaning and using up more context.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nMore tokens means more cost, more latency and running out of context sooner, and long-document quality may also suffer. When building products for multilingual markets such as Singapore, this is a real cost and quality issue. When choosing models, measure token counts on samples in the target languages rather than only looking at English benchmarks.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Give the V×d parameter estimate and explain the impact of embeddings being a large share of small models.\n- Remedies for multilingual settings: choose models whose vocabulary covers the target languages, expand the vocabulary and continue training, estimate cost from actual token counts.","L1-1.3.1":"The embedding layer maps token ids to vectors, and the LM head maps the final hidden state back to logits over the vocabulary; weight tying makes the two share the same matrix.\n\n**How it works**\n- The input embedding is a lookup table `E` of shape V×d, equivalent to a bias-free linear layer applied to a one-hot vector; it's context-free — the same id always retrieves the same vector.\n- Output layer: the final hidden state is multiplied by `W_U ∈ R^{d×V}` to get logits, then softmaxed into the next-token distribution, i.e. `p(x_{t+1}=v) = softmax(h_t W_U)_v`.\n- Weight tying: set `W_U = Eᵀ`, saving V×d parameters and aligning input and output spaces; used by GPT-2, Gemma and many small models.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nLarge models often don't tie: at tens or hundreds of billions of parameters the saving is negligible, and the input embedding (\"what is this token\") and output embedding (\"what context predicts it\") play different roles, so decoupling gives more freedom. In small models embeddings are a large share, so the impact is big: with V=150k and d=1024, a single matrix is about 150M parameters — 30% of a 500M-parameter model. Also, the LM head does a d×V matrix multiply for every generated token, a non-trivial cost for small models.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- \"Use the LLM's embedding layer for semantic retrieval\" is wrong; retrieval uses purpose-trained embedding models.\n- Be able to compute the parameters saved by tying and their share.","L1-1.3.2":"The original Transformer multiplies token embeddings by √d before adding positional encodings; modern RoPE-based models no longer need this step.\n\n**How it works**\n- In the original design, sinusoidal positional encodings take values in [-1, 1], while embeddings are usually small after initialization. Multiplying by √d brings the embeddings to a magnitude comparable to the positional encodings, so that after addition neither the positional signal nor the semantic signal overwhelms the other.\n- With weight tying, the embedding also serves as the output matrix and its initialization scale is small, so the scaling also adjusts the input magnitude (a common explanation).\n- RoPE doesn't add position vectors to the input; it rotates q and k inside each attention layer, so there is no longer a \"match the magnitude of positional encodings\" problem at the input, and the scaling is unnecessary. A few models, such as Gemma, still keep it.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nIt's a classic detail whose design motivation lapsed as the architecture changed: you'll see it when reading old code or reproducing papers, and understanding why lets you judge whether a new model needs it. Whether you copy it when hand-writing a model affects numeric scales at initialization.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Two sentences suffice: to match the magnitude of the positional encodings; RoPE models add no position vectors so it's unnecessary, though Gemma keeps it.","L1-1.3.3":"The logit lens is an interpretability tool: multiply an intermediate layer's hidden state directly by the output matrix `W_U` to see what the model \"would predict right now\" at that layer.\n\n**How it works**\n- In a residual architecture, each layer \"adds\" something to the same residual stream rather than transforming the representation into a new space, so hidden states across layers live in roughly the same representation space.\n- So you can take layer l's hidden state (usually after the final norm), multiply by `W_U` to get logits, and watch how the top token or the target token's probability evolves across layers.\n- A common observation: shallow layers predict something close to the input token or frequent words, and deeper layers get progressively closer to the final output, letting you see \"at which layer the answer forms\".\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nIt's a foundational tool for studying a model's internal computation, and it also corroborates the residual-stream view: removing one layer usually causes only mild degradation, not collapse. The limitation is that intermediate representations aren't necessarily perfectly aligned with the last layer, so direct readings can be biased; later improvements train a small mapping for each layer (general background).\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Explain the precondition for the logit lens: the residual stream + a shared representation space.\n- Use it as a debugging tool: locate at which layer a fact or a wrong answer appears.","L1-1.3.4":"Scaled dot-product attention answers one question: how much information should the current position take from which positions in the sequence? The formula is `softmax(QKᵀ/√d_k + M)V`.\n\n**How it works**\n- Project each position's vector into three roles: Query, \"what am I looking for\"; Key, \"what do I have here\"; Value, \"what I'll give you if you pick me\".\n- Element (i, j) of `QKᵀ` is the dot product of position i's query with position j's key, i.e. a relevance score; divide by √d_k to stabilize the scale; add the mask `M` (0 for allowed positions, −∞ for forbidden ones); softmax row-wise into weights; finally take the weighted sum over V.\n- Here `Q, K ∈ R^{T×d_k}` and `V ∈ R^{T×d_v}`.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nThree fundamental advantages over RNNs: the information path between any two positions is O(1) (O(n) for RNNs); all positions can be computed in parallel during training; relevance is determined by content rather than distance. The cost is O(n²·d) compute per layer and O(n²) memory for the attention matrix in a naive implementation.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Be able to hand-code a multi-head implementation with a causal mask; common bugs: forgetting to divide by √d_head (the per-head dimension, not d_model), applying the mask after softmax, getting the reshape and transpose order wrong.\n- Be able to explain the intuition behind the Q/K/V roles.","L1-1.3.5":"In self-attention, the same token's input x goes through three different linear projections to get q, k and v, which differ in both values and responsibilities. Splitting them lets \"what to match on\" and \"what to move\" be learned independently.\n\n**How it works**\n- Decoupling matching from moving: the scores depend only on `W_Q·W_Kᵀ` (rank at most d_head), which decides \"whom to look at\" — the QK circuit; the output depends only on `W_V·W_O`, which decides \"what to move and which direction of the residual stream to write it to\" — the OV circuit.\n- Without projections (Q = K = x), the score matrix `xxᵀ` is symmetric, and each token's dot product with itself is usually the largest, so it mostly attends to itself; with two different projections the symmetry breaks, and \"verb looks for object\" can differ from \"object looks for verb\".\n- Projections provide learnable subspaces, so different heads can each learn their own relations.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nIf `W_Q = W_K`, the score matrix is symmetric positive semidefinite and loses directionality, while most linguistic dependencies are asymmetric (modifier → head word, pronoun → antecedent). If v used x directly, the output would just be a weighted average of x, unable to change the representation of what gets moved.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Implementation: the three projections are fused into one GEMM; under GQA the fused width is `(n_h + 2·n_kv)·d_head`.\n- The same token acts both as a key being retrieved and as a query initiating retrieval, so it needs two different vectors.","L1-1.3.6":"Mathematically, attention is a differentiable, content-addressed soft dictionary lookup: it makes a soft selection based on the similarity between the query and each key, and retrieves a weighted average of the values.\n\n**How it works**\n- Form: `o_i = Σ_j α_ij v_j`, `α_i = softmax_j(q_i·k_j / √d_k)`. The weights are non-negative and sum to 1.\n- Equivalent view: kernel smoothing (Nadaraya–Watson) with `exp(q·k/√d)` as the kernel, so the output is always a convex combination of values.\n- Difference from linear layers and convolutions: the mixing coefficients aren't fixed parameters but computed dynamically from the input — an \"input-dependent linear operator\" that routes information by content, with any two positions reachable in one step.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nThis view directly reveals the limitations: a convex combination can only interpolate within the span of V, so new features have to come from the FFN and residuals; softmax is a zero-sum competition — with n tokens, for a relevant token to get half the weight its logit must exceed the rest by about `ln(n−1)`, roughly 13.8 for n of one million, which is why attention gets diluted over long contexts; stacking pure attention collapses to rank 1 at a doubly exponential rate (rank collapse), which residuals and MLPs prevent.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Removing softmax and applying feature maps to q and k gives linear attention, which loses sharp selection.\n- Attention weights can't be taken directly as explanations; verify with occlusion / ablation.","L1-1.3.7":"Dividing by √d_k prevents dot products from growing with dimension and pushing softmax into saturation, where gradients vanish. It's the single most frequently asked attention interview question.\n\n**How it works**\n- Dot-product variance grows linearly with dimension: if the components of q and k are independent with mean 0 and variance 1, then `q·k = Σ q_i k_i` has mean 0, variance d_k and standard deviation √d_k.\n- Large logits → softmax saturation: at d_k = 1024 typical logit magnitudes are on the order of ±32, making softmax nearly one-hot.\n- Saturation → vanishing gradients: the softmax Jacobian is `diag(p) − ppᵀ`, whose entries go to 0 as p approaches one-hot.\n- Dividing by √d_k brings the variance back to 1, making the logit scale independent of dimension. Measured at d_k=1024: variance about 1027, about 1.0 after scaling; the largest weight is about 0.9999 unscaled and about 0.13 scaled.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nWhy √d_k rather than d_k: what needs normalizing is the standard deviation; dividing by d_k squashes logits too much, making softmax nearly uniform, which also fails to learn discrimination. The assumption only holds at initialization — after training q and k are no longer independent with unit variance — which is why QK-Norm later appeared to constrain the logit scale throughout.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Walk through the four-step chain: variance → saturation → vanishing gradients → scaling.\n- When hand-coding, use the per-head d_head, not d_model.","L1-1.3.8":"Additive attention (Bahdanau attention) is the attention form used in early seq2seq models. It scores query and key with a small MLP, in contrast to the Transformer's dot-product attention.\n\n**How it works**\n- The scoring function is roughly `score(q, k) = vᵀ tanh(W_1 q + W_2 k)`: linearly map q and k, add them, apply tanh, then project to a scalar (the general form).\n- Thanks to tanh's bounded activation and the learnable projection, the score's magnitude doesn't grow with dimension, so there's no need to divide by √d_k as in dot-product attention.\n- Dot-product attention uses `q·k` directly, whose variance grows with d_k, so it must be scaled.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nDot-product attention won on engineering efficiency: `QKᵀ` computes all position pairs at once with highly optimized matrix multiplication, far faster on GPUs than running each pair through an MLP, and with √d_k scaling the quality is just as good. Additive attention in principle doesn't suffer from scale issues at large d_k, but its computational form doesn't parallelize well at scale.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- If asked \"why doesn't additive attention need scaling?\": scores come from a small MLP whose magnitude doesn't grow with dimension.\n- \"Why dot products?\": they use highly optimized matrix multiplies and are hardware-friendly.","L1-1.3.9":"Multi-head attention splits the d-dimensional space into h heads, runs attention independently in each, concatenates the results and applies an output projection, gaining multiple attention subspaces for the same compute.\n\n**How it works**\n- Each head has dimension `d_head = d / h`, `head_i = Attention(XW_Q^(i), XW_K^(i), XW_V^(i))`, `MHA(X) = Concat(head_1..head_h) W_O`.\n- Parameters: `W_Q, W_K, W_V, W_O` are each d×d, 4d² in total (excluding biases), independent of the number of heads.\n- Compute is essentially the same as \"one head, full dimension\", since h heads of d/h dimensions keep the total dimension unchanged.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nOne softmax can only produce one attention distribution, yet a token often needs to attend to several relations at once (syntactic subject, coreferent antecedent, adjacent positions); a single head can only average them together, while multiple heads model them separately — effectively free extra subspaces. The cost is smaller per-head dimension and thus less expressiveness per head; at inference each head has its own K/V, so the KV cache grows with the number of heads, which is exactly what MQA / GQA address.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- \"Why multiple heads?\": multiple attention distributions at unchanged compute.\n- \"Do more heads add parameters?\": no, it's always 4d².\n- When hand-coding, mind the reshape / transpose order and swap the dimensions back before concatenating.","L1-1.3.10":"In a trained multi-head attention model, different heads specialize into different functions, yet there's also a lot of redundancy: pruning a sizable fraction of heads barely hurts performance.\n\n**How it works**\n- Specialization: empirically one observes positional heads (attending to a fixed relative position, such as the previous token), syntactic heads (attending to dependency relations), induction heads (recognizing the \"A B … A\" pattern and predicting B — an important mechanism behind in-context learning), and more.\n- Redundancy: Michel et al. (2019) found that pruning a sizable fraction of heads at inference barely reduces performance, and many layers work even with a single head.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nTogether these show that the value of multiple heads lies more in providing many optimizable paths during training, making good solutions easier to find, than in every head being indispensable at inference. In engineering terms, this justifies head pruning and MQA / GQA sharing K/V — given the redundancy, reducing the number of K/V heads costs little quality.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Be able to cite induction heads and explain their relation to in-context learning.\n- \"If heads are redundant, why not train with fewer?\": multiple heads mainly aid optimization, and fewer heads may be harder to train; at inference, prune or use GQA to cut cost.","L1-1.3.11":"Self-attention and cross-attention differ in where Q, K and V come from; the causal mask lets position i see only j ≤ i and is the foundation of decoder-only models.\n\n**How it works**\n- Self-attention: Q, K and V come from the same sequence.\n- Cross-attention: Q comes from one sequence (e.g. the decoder's current state) and K, V from another (e.g. the encoder output); used in encoder-decoders, some multimodal models (text \"looking at\" image features) and text conditioning in diffusion models.\n- Causal mask: a matrix with −∞ in the upper triangle, added before softmax.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nThe causal mask has three important consequences:\n- Parallel training: one forward pass over a length-T sequence yields next-token losses at all T positions (teacher forcing), so every token provides supervision.\n- The KV cache works: position i's representation depends only on tokens ≤ i, so when a new token arrives the K and V of earlier positions don't change and can be cached; bidirectional attention lacks this property.\n- Implicit positional information: position i can see exactly i tokens, so even without positional encodings the model isn't fully permutation invariant — the starting point of NoPE-style work.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- The mask must be applied before softmax; verify causality by checking that changing the last token doesn't affect earlier outputs.","L1-1.3.12":"Attention costs O(n²·d) time per layer, the root reason long contexts are expensive; but counting FLOPs precisely, attention only exceeds the cost of projections and the FFN once n reaches about 6d.\n\n**How it works**\n- Self-attention: n² (query, key) pairs, one d-dimensional dot product each, O(n²·d) per layer; a naive implementation needs O(n²) attention-matrix memory per head.\n- FFN: O(n·d²) per layer, linear in sequence length.\n- Generating one new token (with a KV cache): O(n·d) attention + O(d²) FFN; generating n tokens costs O(n²·d) attention in total, and O(n³·d) without a cache.\n- Precise per-layer forward FLOPs (standard MHA + 4×-wide FFN): projections about 8nd², FFN about 16nd², attention about 4n²d, crossing over at about n ≈ 6d.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nModern LLMs have d of 4k–8k, so attention only becomes the compute bottleneck beyond about 25k tokens at d = 4096 or about 50k tokens at d = 8192; but bandwidth pressure from reading the KV cache during decode arrives earlier. Going from 100k to 1M context multiplies attention compute by 100, not 10 — the motivation for everyone reworking attention architectures in 2025–2026.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Be able to write out each complexity term and derive the n ≈ 6d crossover.\n- Distinguish compute bottlenecks from bandwidth bottlenecks.","L1-1.4.1":"Attention itself is permutation equivariant: shuffle the input order and the output just shuffles along, so the model has no way to know what comes before what. Positional information must therefore be injected separately.\n\n**How it works**\n- Attention scores depend only on the content of q and k, and the weighted sum is order-independent, so without positional information \"the cat chases the dog\" and \"the dog chases the cat\" yield the same set of representations.\n- There are four mainstream injection methods: sinusoidal (fixed vectors added to the input), learned (a trainable absolute position table), RoPE (rotating q/k in every layer) and ALiBi (adding a distance bias to the scores). The first two are absolute and injected once at the input layer; the latter two express relative position and are injected in every layer.\n- The causal mask also implicitly leaks position: position i sees exactly i tokens, so causal models aren't fully permutation invariant even without positional encodings.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nThe choice of positional encoding determines whether the model can express relative position, whether it can extrapolate to lengths unseen in training, and whether it's compatible with the KV cache. What really matters in language is mostly relative position, which drove the evolution from absolute encodings to RoPE.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Use the term \"permutation equivariant\" to state the reason precisely.\n- Compare the four schemes along: absolute / relative, additive / multiplicative, where injected, parameters, extrapolation.","L1-1.4.2":"Sinusoidal and learned encodings are two kinds of absolute positional encoding: the former uses fixed multi-frequency sine/cosine vectors, the latter a trainable position table. Both are added to the embeddings at the input layer, and both extrapolate poorly.\n\n**How it works**\n- Sinusoidal (original Transformer): `PE(pos,2i) = sin(pos / 10000^(2i/d))`, `PE(pos,2i+1) = cos(pos / 10000^(2i/d))`. Different dimensions have geometrically spaced wavelengths from 2π to 10000·2π, like clock hands turning at many different speeds. The design motivation is that for any offset k, `PE(pos+k)` can be expressed as a linear transformation (rotation) of `PE(pos)`, making relative position easier to learn.\n- Learned (BERT, GPT-2): a trainable `max_len × d` matrix where position i looks up row i; length is hard-coded at max_len (512 for BERT, 1024 for GPT-2).\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nSinusoidal has no parameters but in practice doesn't extrapolate well beyond training length; learned is simple and effective but has no vector for positions beyond max_len, so it can't extrapolate at all. A shared problem: what matters in language is mostly relative position, yet absolute encodings force the model to infer \"adjacent\" from the vectors for \"position 17\" and \"position 18\"; and since they're injected only once at the input, they get diluted in deeper layers.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Be able to write the sinusoidal formula and explain the intuition behind the multi-frequency design.\n- Explain why both were replaced by RoPE.","L1-1.4.3":"RoPE (Rotary Position Embedding, Su et al. 2021) rotates q and k by an angle determined by their own positions before attention in every layer, so attention scores depend only on relative distance. It has been the choice of almost every mainstream LLM since 2023.\n\n**How it works**\n- Treat the d-dimensional vector as d/2 complex numbers formed from pairs of dimensions; pair i at position m is rotated by `m·θ_i`, with `θ_i = 10000^(−2i/d)`.\n- In complex notation, the query at position m is `q·e^{imθ}` and the key at position n is `k·e^{inθ}`; the real part of their inner product is `Re[q·conj(k)·e^{i(m−n)θ}]`, which depends only on m − n: the rotation applied is absolute, but the score reflects relative position.\n- Implementation: `x1*cos − x2*sin` and `x1*sin + x2*cos` applied to adjacent dimension pairs.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nWhy it became mainstream: it's relative and acts multiplicatively on the scores, re-injected in every layer so it isn't diluted; zero extra parameters, and rotation preserves norms; it applies only to q and k, not v, and the cache stores already-rotated k, so it's KV-cache compatible; there are mature techniques for length extension. Limitations: high-frequency components alias in phase over very long distances, so extrapolation isn't unlimited; it requires pairwise dot products, which constrains compressed / sparse attention — MLA, for instance, needs decoupled RoPE.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Be able to prove \"depends only on m − n\" with the complex-number derivation.\n- Explain what raising the base does (Llama 3 went from 10000 to 500000).","L1-1.4.4":"RoPE length extension is a family of techniques that let a model trained only on short sequences handle longer contexts. The core idea is to adjust rotation frequencies so that relative distances at inference fall back into the range of angles seen during training.\n\n**How it works**\n- The problem: training only saw sequences of length ≤ N, so at inference, longer relative distances produce RoPE rotation angles in an unseen range.\n- Raising the base: slows down rotation in every dimension, so the same relative distance maps to a smaller angle; Llama 3 raised the base from 10000 to 500000.\n- Position Interpolation: scale position indices down proportionally so long sequences map back into the training-length range.\n- NTK-aware scaling: treat frequency components differently, extending low frequencies while preserving high-frequency resolution.\n- YaRN: scale by frequency band and adjust the attention temperature.\n- The more fundamental fix: continue training on long sequences.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nThese methods can extend context several-fold at modest cost, but they only solve the \"can recognize positions\" layer; whether the model can actually use long context also depends on compute and memory, and on effective utilization. Some hybrid architectures drop positional encoding in global layers or use proportional RoPE variants to improve long-range generalization.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Be able to answer long-context questions in layers: positions → compute and memory → effective utilization.\n- Note that extension methods usually need to be paired with a small amount of continued training on long text.","L1-1.4.5":"ALiBi (Attention with Linear Biases, Press et al. 2021) neither changes the embeddings nor rotates vectors; instead it subtracts a penalty proportional to distance directly from the attention scores, achieving strong length extrapolation.\n\n**How it works**\n- Scoring: `score(i, j) = q_i·k_j − m_h·(i − j)`, for j ≤ i.\n- `m_h` is a fixed, unlearned slope per head; with 8 heads it's the geometric sequence 1/2, 1/4, …, 1/256.\n- The effect is a built-in recency bias: the farther a token, the costlier it is to attend to, and different heads have different \"radii of vision\".\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nThe selling point is good length extrapolation: train on 1k and it still works at 2k and beyond, with no extra parameters. The downside is systematic suppression of distant information, making it worse than RoPE on tasks requiring precise long-range retrieval. Few mainstream LLMs use it today — representative models are BLOOM and MPT — but the idea of \"adding a positional bias to the scores\" still appears in many attention variants.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Compared with RoPE: ALiBi is an additive bias that extrapolates well but suppresses distant information; RoPE is a multiplicative rotation with moderate but extensible extrapolation and better long-range retrieval.\n- Explain why slopes differ per head: so different heads cover different distance scales.","L1-1.4.6":"Positional encoding saw new directions in 2025–2026: the global attention layers of hybrid architectures sometimes drop positional encoding entirely (NoPE), and some models try other relative-position schemes in place of RoPE.\n\n**How it works**\n- NoPE: global layers add no positional encoding at all, relying on the positional information implicit in the causal mask and on the order information supplied by neighboring local-attention / recurrent layers; e.g. the global layers of Arcee Trinity Large.\n- iRoPE (Llama 4 Scout): interleaved attention layers use no positional encoding, and attention is temperature-scaled at inference to improve length generalization.\n- Gemma 4's global layers use Proportional RoPE (p-RoPE); the model card says the goal is better memory efficiency.\n- Thinking Machines' Inkling (2026-07-15, a 975B MoE): officially said to replace RoPE with relative position embeddings, claiming better extrapolation to longer sequences; see its technical materials for implementation details.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nThe motivation is long context: RoPE's extrapolation has a ceiling and constrains compressed / sparse attention. Freeing the global layers responsible for precise long-range retrieval from positional encoding while local layers handle order is a division of labor. The cost is that these schemes are still new and their effectiveness depends on the overall architecture.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Explain why NoPE works: the causal mask itself leaks position.\n- Knowing names like iRoPE and p-RoPE earns bonus points; note that these are official claims.","L1-1.5.1":"Pre-LN and Post-LN differ in whether normalization sits inside the residual branch or after the residual addition. Since GPT-2 the mainstream has used Pre-LN because deep training is more stable.\n\n**How it works**\n- Post-LN (original Transformer, BERT): `x_{l+1} = LN(x_l + F(x_l))`.\n- Pre-LN: `x_{l+1} = x_l + F(LN(x_l))`.\n- In Pre-LN there is a completely untransformed identity path from output to input, so gradients reach any shallow layer without decay and gradient norms are roughly uniform across layers. In Post-LN every layer's output passes through an LN, breaking the identity path; layers near the output get oversized gradients, training tends to diverge early, a carefully designed warmup is essential, and deep stacks become very hard to train (Xiong et al., 2020).\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nThe cost: Pre-LN's residual stream isn't normalized and its values accumulate with depth, so a final norm must be added after the last layer before the LM head — often forgotten when hand-writing models. Some research suggests a successfully trained Post-LN may perform slightly better, as Pre-LN dilutes the contribution of deep layers; but in a scale-first era, stability trumps everything.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Explain from the gradient path why Pre-LN is more stable.\n- Mention the easy-to-miss final norm.","L1-1.5.2":"Sandwich norm places a normalization both before and after each sublayer. The Gemma series uses it, and Meta's Muse Glimmer also adopts Gemma-style pre- and post-normalization.\n\n**How it works**\n- Standard Pre-LN: `x + F(Norm(x))`, normalizing only at the sublayer input.\n- Sandwich: add another RMSNorm before the sublayer output is written back to the residual stream, roughly `x + Norm_post(F(Norm_pre(x)))`.\n- This also bounds the magnitude of the increment each sublayer writes into the residual stream, preventing some layers' outputs from growing too large and accumulating in the stream until values blow up.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nIt tries to get the best of both structures: keep Pre-LN's identity gradient path (the residual trunk still bypasses normalization) while, like Post-LN, regulating each layer's output magnitude so layer contributions are more balanced. The cost is an extra set of normalization compute and parameters (small). It shows that normalization placement is still an active engineering tuning knob, not a settled question.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- When asked \"is Pre-LN always best?\", cite sandwich norm as a compromise.\n- Be able to draw where normalization sits in Post-LN, Pre-LN and sandwich.","L1-1.5.3":"LayerNorm both centers and rescales, while RMSNorm only rescales; RMSNorm performs comparably at lower cost and has been the default since Llama. BatchNorm, which depends on batch statistics, doesn't fit Transformers.\n\n**How it works**\n- `LayerNorm(x) = (x − μ) / sqrt(σ² + ε) ⊙ γ + β`.\n- `RMSNorm(x) = x / sqrt(mean(x²) + ε) ⊙ γ`, dropping the mean and the bias β (Zhang & Sennrich, 2019). The hypothesis is that what makes LayerNorm work is scale invariance, and centering contributes little.\n- Both normalize over a single token's feature dimension, independent of batch and sequence length, with identical behavior in training and inference.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nWhy not BatchNorm: it relies on batch-dimension statistics, but sequence lengths vary and padding pollutes the statistics; large-model training has tiny per-GPU batches; and in autoregressive inference batch statistics don't match training (batch statistics in training, running averages at inference). RMSNorm saves one mean computation and one parameter vector, savings that add up in large-scale training.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Write both formulas and point out the differences.\n- For \"why not BatchNorm\", give three reasons: variable length / padding, small batches, train-inference mismatch.","L1-1.5.4":"QK-Norm (also called QK-layernorm) normalizes q and k separately before the dot product and multiplies by a learnable scale, directly capping the magnitude of attention logits and improving training stability.\n\n**How it works**\n- Apply RMSNorm or L2 normalization to q and k before the dot product, paired with a learnable temperature / scale.\n- Motivation: during training `qᵀk` keeps growing, pushing softmax into saturation so attention becomes nearly one-hot; gradients vanish first, then some position flips and causes a loss spike. Wortsman et al. reproduced this instability in small models with high learning rates and verified that QK-layernorm fixes it. √d_k only keeps the scale reasonable at initialization, whereas QK-Norm constrains it throughout.\n- Adopters: Gemma 3, Qwen3, OLMo 3 and others; the Qwen3 report cites training stability as the reason.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nThere are counterexamples too: Cohere's Tiny Aya (2026-02) removed QK-Norm, reportedly because it may hurt long-context performance. A speculative explanation is that normalized dot products are bounded, making it hard to concentrate attention sharply enough on a single position in long sequences. This is a classic \"stability vs long-range retrieval sharpness\" trade-off.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Connect QK-Norm to the \"growing attention logits\" mechanism behind loss spikes.\n- Know that Kimi K2, when using Muon, observed max logits above 1000 and proposed QK-Clip as an alternative constraint.","L1-1.5.5":"The FFN is the per-position feed-forward network in the second half of each block: attention moves information between positions, the FFN processes information at a single position, and it is believed to store a large share of factual knowledge.\n\n**How it works**\n- Standard form: `FFN(x) = W_2 σ(W_1 x)`, with `W_1 ∈ R^{4d×d}` and `W_2 ∈ R^{d×4d}`; positions don't interact.\n- Key-value memory view (Geva et al., 2021): each row of `W_1` is a \"pattern detector\" (key) which, when activated, writes the corresponding column of `W_2` (value) back to the residual stream. A substantial share of factual knowledge is stored in FFN weights.\n- Parameters: in the standard configuration the FFN has 8d² and attention 4d², so the FFN is about two-thirds of block parameters.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nThe large parameter share explains why MoE turns the FFN, not attention, into \"experts\": enlarging FFN capacity adds the most knowledge capacity, while routing per token activates only a few experts and keeps compute in check. The key-value memory view is also the basis of knowledge-editing methods (locating and modifying specific FFN weights).\n\n**Interview notes**\n- \"What do attention and the FFN each do?\": moving vs processing.\n- Be able to compute the FFN-to-attention parameter ratio and use it to explain MoE's design choice.","L1-1.5.6":"Activation functions in Transformer FFNs evolved from ReLU → GELU → SiLU / gated variants (SwiGLU, GeGLU), trending toward smoother and gated.\n\n**How it works**\n- ReLU: `max(0, x)`, simple; zero gradient on the negative half-axis can produce \"dead neurons\". Used by the original Transformer.\n- GELU: `x·Φ(x)`, where Φ is the standard normal CDF; smooth with small non-zero outputs on the negative side, interpretable as the expectation of \"randomly keep the input according to its size\". Used by BERT and GPT-2/3.\n- SiLU / Swish: `x·sigmoid(x)`, shaped much like GELU, and a component of SwiGLU.\n- SwiGLU: `W_2(SiLU(W_1 x) ⊙ W_3 x)`, one branch activated, one branch as a gate, multiplied elementwise; used by Llama, Qwen, DeepSeek, Mistral.\n- GeGLU: also gated, with GELU as the activation; used by Gemma.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nSmooth activations make optimization smoother, and gating lets the network multiplicatively modulate each hidden unit based on input content; empirically this consistently beats GELU at the same parameter count. Gating adds a matrix, so the hidden dimension must shrink to keep the parameter count.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Write each activation's formula and name representative models in chronological order.\n- Explain ReLU's dead-neuron problem and GELU's probabilistic intuition.","L1-1.5.7":"SwiGLU is a gated FFN from Shazeer's 2020 study of GLU variants and is now standard in Llama, Qwen, DeepSeek, Mistral and others; it adds a matrix, so the hidden dimension shrinks to 8d/3 to keep the parameter count unchanged.\n\n**How it works**\n- Formula: `FFN(x) = W_2(SiLU(W_1 x) ⊙ W_3 x)`. The `W_1` branch goes through SiLU, the `W_3` branch acts as a linear \"gate\", the two are multiplied elementwise, and `W_2` projects back to d dimensions.\n- Parameters: a standard FFN has two matrices and a 4d hidden layer, 8d² in total; SwiGLU has three matrices, which would be 12d² with a 4d hidden layer. Shrinking the hidden layer to `(2/3)·4d = 8d/3` gives `3 × d × 8d/3 = 8d²`, the same as a standard FFN.\n- Real configs round 8d/3 to a hardware-friendly multiple, e.g. Llama-3-8B's d_ff is 14336.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nGating lets the network multiplicatively modulate each hidden unit by input content, and empirically it consistently beats GELU at the same parameter count. The costs are three matrix multiplies, a slightly more complex implementation, and gate components that can produce outliers in MoE training (DeepSeek-V4 truncates them with SwiGLU Clamping).\n\n**Interview notes**\n- \"Why isn't Llama's FFN hidden size 4d?\": this is testing the 8d/3 derivation.\n- Be able to write the formula and explain what each branch does.","L1-1.5.8":"The residual connection `x_{l+1} = x_l + F(x_l)` is the key to training deep networks. The classic explanation is the \"gradient highway\": during backpropagation, gradients can travel straight to shallow layers along the identity path.\n\n**How it works**\n- Differentiating `x_{l+1} = x_l + F(x_l)` gives `∂x_{l+1}/∂x_l = I + ∂F/∂x_l`. Even when `∂F/∂x_l` is small, the identity term I prevents the gradient from decaying through repeated multiplication across layers.\n- Stacked over many layers, there is a path from the loss to any shallow layer that passes only through additions, which mitigates vanishing gradients and makes networks with hundreds of layers trainable.\n- Combined with Pre-LN, the trunk path passes through no normalization or nonlinearity at all, giving the smoothest gradient flow.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nResiduals have a deeper meaning too: layers don't \"replace\" the representation with a new one but write increments into the same space, which leads to the residual-stream view and interpretability tools such as the logit lens. They also prevent the rank collapse of stacked pure attention. The costs: under Pre-LN, residual-stream values accumulate with depth and need a final norm; in deep stacks, early signals are diluted by equal-weight accumulation, which drove 2026 modifications such as mHC and Attention Residuals.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Use the derivative to show why gradients don't vanish.\n- Be able to tie residual connections to Pre-LN, rank collapse and the residual stream.","L1-1.5.9":"The residual stream view (Elhage et al., 2021) treats the x running through all layers as a \"bus\" of width d: each layer reads from the bus and adds its result back.\n\n**How it works**\n- The embedding writes the initial content onto the bus; every attention head and every FFN reads from the bus through a linear projection, computes, and adds the result back.\n- Layers don't transform the representation into a new one layer by layer; they keep writing increments into the same space.\n- So intermediate states can be read directly with the LM head (logit lens), and removing a layer usually causes only mild degradation rather than collapse.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nThis view explains many phenomena: Pre-LN makes sense because each sublayer normalizes before reading (it reads the direction) and writing back doesn't destroy what's already on the bus; different heads can write information into different subspaces of the bus for later layers to read, forming \"circuits\"; and it explains why layer pruning and early exit work. It's the foundational framework for mechanistic interpretability research.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Describe each sublayer's role as \"read — add back\", not \"transform\".\n- Name the phenomena that follow: the logit lens works, removing layers doesn't break things, Pre-LN is justified.","L1-1.5.10":"2026 brought two modifications to the standard residual connection: DeepSeek's mHC mixes multiple residual streams under constraints, and Moonshot's Attention Residuals aggregate along the depth dimension with attention.\n\n**How it works**\n- mHC (Manifold-Constrained Hyper-Connections): proposed by DeepSeek in December 2025, it expands the single residual stream into several and learns a mixing matrix between them, while constraining the mixing matrix to a particular manifold to preserve the stability that comes from the identity mapping. Used in DeepSeek-V4.\n- Attention Residuals (AttnRes): proposed by Moonshot in March 2026, it replaces fixed layer-by-layer accumulation with attention along the depth dimension, letting each layer selectively aggregate the outputs of earlier layers based on input content. Used in Kimi K3.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nShared motivation: the standard residual is an equal-weight sum of all previous layers' outputs, and in very deep networks early signals get diluted; making the aggregation learnable improves signal propagation at the same depth and parameter count. The costs are a more complex implementation, extra compute, and the need to carefully preserve the stability of the identity path — which is exactly why mHC adds the manifold constraint.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- A bonus point: knowing they exist and stating the motivation is enough.\n- Be able to connect them to \"residual = gradient highway\" and \"deep-layer contributions get diluted\".","L1-1.6.1":"MQA and GQA compress the KV cache by having multiple query heads share K/V: in MQA all heads share one set, while GQA groups heads and shares within each group — a compromise between MHA and MQA and now standard in modern LLMs.\n\n**How it works**\n- Cached elements per token per layer: MHA `2·n_h·d_head`; MQA (Shazeer 2019) `2·d_head`, the smallest cache but with quality loss; GQA (Ainslie et al. 2023) `2·n_kv·d_head`.\n- Key observation: Q heads need diversity (each head asks a different question), while K/V are highly redundant and can be shared.\n- Example: Llama 3 8B uses 32 query heads and 8 KV heads, making the KV cache 1/4 of MHA with almost no quality loss. In 2026 Meta's Muse Glimmer 30B went further, pairing 32 query heads with 2 KV heads.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nGQA also reduces K and V projection parameters, but that's minor; the core gain is inference memory and memory bandwidth — during decode the entire KV cache is read for every generated token, so a smaller cache means more concurrency and longer context with less bandwidth pressure. Too few KV heads hurts quality; n_kv is the quality-vs-cost knob.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Be able to compute the KV cache from a config: `2 × L × n_kv × d_head × bytes`, e.g. 128 KiB per token for Llama-3-8B in BF16.\n- Distinguish the parameter savings (small) from the inference memory / bandwidth savings (large).","L1-1.6.2":"MLA (Multi-head Latent Attention) is the KV cache compression scheme introduced by DeepSeek-V2: rather than reducing the number of heads, it jointly low-rank-compresses K and V into a low-dimensional latent and caches only that latent.\n\n**How it works**\n- Each token in each layer caches only a latent vector c of dimension d_c (far smaller than `n_h·d_head`), and each head's K and V are recovered through up-projection matrices when needed; the per-token cache is `d_c + d_R`.\n- Matrix absorption: the up-projection matrices can be \"absorbed\" into subsequent matrix multiplies (e.g. merged into W_Q or W_O), so K and V never need to be explicitly reconstructed at inference.\n- The hard part is RoPE: the rotation matrix depends on position, and sitting in the middle it blocks absorption. The fix is decoupled RoPE — set aside a small number of dimensions dedicated to carrying positional information (one RoPE-bearing key component shared by all heads), with the remaining dimensions taking the compressed path.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nThe DeepSeek-V2 paper reports that MLA's cache size is equivalent to GQA with only 2.25 groups, while performing better than MHA. It was later adopted by DeepSeek-V3, Kimi K2 / K2.5, Mistral Large 3, GLM-5 and others. The cost is implementation complexity, and compatibility with RoPE needs extra design.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Compared with GQA: GQA reduces the number of heads, MLA does low-rank compression.\n- Explain \"why RoPE blocks matrix absorption and how decoupled RoPE fixes it\".","L1-1.6.3":"The real bottleneck of standard attention on GPUs is often not floating-point operations but memory reads and writes (memory I/O): a naive implementation writes the N×N intermediate matrix to HBM and reads it back.\n\n**How it works**\n- A naive implementation runs step by step: compute `S = QKᵀ` and write it to HBM → read it back, apply softmax to get P and write that to HBM → read P back and multiply by V. Both the N×N score matrix and the probability matrix make full round trips through memory.\n- GPU compute has grown far faster than memory bandwidth, and elementwise operations such as softmax and masking have very low arithmetic intensity, so most of the time is spent waiting for data movement.\n- GPUs have small but extremely fast on-chip SRAM and large but slower HBM; the key is to keep data in SRAM while computing as much as possible.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nThis insight is the starting point of FlashAttention: without changing the math, an IO-aware implementation that cuts HBM traffic delivers several-fold speedups and reduces extra memory from O(N²) to O(N). It also shows that performance analysis must distinguish compute-bound from memory-bound work instead of just counting FLOPs; reading the KV cache during decode is likewise a classic bandwidth bottleneck.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Explain the hierarchy difference between HBM and SRAM.\n- The standard answer to \"why is attention slow?\": memory traffic, not FLOPs.","L1-1.6.4":"FlashAttention (Dao et al., 2022) computes exact standard attention, numerically equivalent to the naive implementation, but its IO-aware implementation cuts extra memory from O(N²) to O(N) and runs several times faster.\n\n**How it works**\n- Tiling: split Q, K and V into small blocks, load each into the GPU's on-chip SRAM, complete \"dot product → softmax → multiply by V\" within the block in one go, and write only the final output back to HBM.\n- Online softmax: softmax needs the normalization constant for an entire row; when processing block by block, keep two running statistics — the current max and the current sum — and rescale the existing result whenever a new block is read, so the final result is exact.\n- Backward recomputation: don't store the N×N intermediate matrices; recompute them from the saved statistics.\n- Evolution: FA-2 (2023) cut non-matmul operations and improved the parallel partitioning, roughly another 2× faster; FA-3 (2024) targeted H100 with warp specialization, asynchronous interleaving and FP8; FA-4 (2026-03) targets Blackwell, reporting up to 1605 TFLOPs/s in BF16 (71% utilization).\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nIt made long-sequence training feasible and is a default component of modern training and inference stacks. Note that it is not an approximation and doesn't change the O(N²) time complexity — it only lowers the constant and the memory; sparse / linear attention are what change the complexity.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- \"Is FlashAttention an approximation?\": no, it's exact.\n- Explain the three pieces: tiling, online softmax, recomputation.","L1-1.6.5":"Sliding-window attention lets each token see only the last W tokens; local-global interleaving uses sliding windows in most layers and global attention in a few — the mainstream form in 2025–2026.\n\n**How it works**\n- Sliding window: per-layer complexity drops from O(N²) to O(N·W), and the KV cache keeps only the last W tokens; information can propagate indirectly through stacked layers, with a theoretical receptive field of about L×W over L layers, though long-range dependencies get diluted. Early representatives: Longformer, BigBird, Mistral 7B.\n- Local-global interleaving: Gemma 3 uses a 5:1 local-to-global ratio; Gemma 4 continues the pattern, with the 31B dense model having 50 local layers + 10 global layers and a 1024-token window in large models. Muse Glimmer 30B and Arcee Trinity Large use 3:1, with Trinity's window at 4096. gpt-oss alternates between a banded window of 128 tokens and fully dense attention, paired with a learnable softmax denominator bias (similar to an attention sink).\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nOnly the global layers need to keep full-length K/V, so the KV cache shrinks dramatically and the memory and bandwidth costs of long context drop significantly. The costs are implementation complexity (different layers have different cache lengths) and possible losses on tasks that \"need precise long-range retrieval in any layer\".\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Be able to estimate the KV cache of a hybrid architecture: local layers only cache tokens inside the window.\n- Cite the local/global ratios of 2–3 models.","L1-1.6.6":"DeepSeek Sparse Attention (DSA) was first released in DeepSeek-V3.2-Exp in September 2025. An extremely lightweight lightning indexer selects the top-k past tokens for each query, and real attention runs only over those.\n\n**How it works**\n- The lightning indexer quickly scores each query against all past tokens and selects only the top-k (the paper specifies k = 2048) for core attention, cutting the core cost from O(N²) to O(N·k).\n- Training has two stages: the indexer first \"warms up\" under dense attention, aligning with the main attention distribution via KL divergence; training then switches to sparse attention and continues.\n- GLM-5 also uses the MLA + DSA combination; DeepSeek-V4's CSA reuses DSA for selection over compressed entries.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nIntuitively, this builds \"retrieval\" into attention itself: the indexer is a learned, per-query retriever. It keeps softmax attention's precise selection while avoiding full computation. Its failure modes resemble retrieval's: if the indexer misses a key token, no amount of downstream attention can recover it. The indexer itself still scores all of history, just very cheaply.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Distinguish it from FlashAttention: DSA changes the amount of computation (complexity); FlashAttention doesn't.\n- Be able to describe how the indexer is trained and its failure modes.","L1-1.6.7":"MiniMax M3 (2026-06-01) uses the in-house MiniMax Sparse Attention (MSA). Officially, per-token compute at 1M context is only 1/20 of the previous generation, making it one of the representatives of the sparse-attention route in 2026.\n\n**How it works**\n- It belongs to the same family of ideas as DeepSeek Sparse Attention: global layers don't make every query attend to all past tokens, but selectively compute only a subset, reducing compute at million-token contexts.\n- See the official technical materials for implementation details; third-party sources say it's built on GQA with about 428B parameters / 23B active, but these numbers are yet to be verified.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nAt million-token contexts, O(N²) attention compute and the KV cache are the main costs, and sparsification is the cost-cutting direction most flagship models have chosen. The costs are the same as other sparse schemes: if the selection mechanism misses key tokens, the information can't be recovered, and precise long-range retrieval tasks need dedicated evaluation. When citing the numbers, state that they are official figures and that the baseline is \"the previous generation\".\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Place MiniMax M3 in the \"sparse attention\" category and compare it alongside DSA, CSA/HCA and linear-attention hybrids.\n- Distinguish verified from unverified information and state the source when citing.","L1-1.6.8":"CSA and HCA are compressed attention mechanisms introduced in DeepSeek-V4 (April 2026): first compress KV along the sequence dimension, then apply sparse or dense attention, reducing compute and cache costs at million-token contexts.\n\n**How it works**\n- CSA (Compressed Sparse Attention): the KV of every m = 4 tokens is merged into 1 entry with learned compression weights; then, reusing DSA, a lightning indexer selects the top-k over the compressed entries (512 for V4-Flash, 1024 for V4-Pro); a separate sliding-window branch with a 128-token window handles the most recent uncompressed tokens, preserving causality and local detail.\n- HCA (Heavily Compressed Attention): a compression ratio of m′ = 128, making the compressed sequence short enough for dense attention directly.\n- The two layer types alternate through the depth (43 layers in V4-Flash, 61 in V4-Pro).\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nOfficial figures: at a million tokens, V4-Pro's per-token inference FLOPs are about 27% of V3.2's and its KV cache about 10%; for V4-Flash, about 10% and 7%. Another figure puts the KV cache at about 2% of an 8-head GQA (BF16) baseline; the two don't contradict each other, they just use different baselines. The cost is that compression may lose fine-grained information such as exact numbers and identifiers.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- When citing numbers, state the baseline.\n- Explain the role of the sliding-window branch in CSA.","L1-1.6.9":"DeepSeek-V4.1-Flash (2026-09-10) squeezes the KV cache another round on top of V4: the global KV cache resident in HBM is about 1/4 of V4-Flash's.\n\n**How it works**\n- Scale: an MoE with 552B total parameters, activating about 8B during prefill and about 16B during decode.\n- Architecture: the model card describes a Causal Encoder-Decoder structure of \"40 Transformer layers = 20 causal encoder layers + 20 decoder layers\".\n- The cost-cutting combination: CSA2 (cross-layer KV cache reuse), FP4 KV cache and single-pass mHC, bringing the global KV cache down to about 890 bytes per token.\n- Other: supports 1M context, MIT license, natively multimodal (in-house DeepSeek-ViT), reasoning effort continuously adjustable from 1–100; officially, from 09-14, API requests for `deepseek-v4-pro` are routed to V4.1-Flash.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nIt shows that several dimensions of KV cache compression can be stacked: compressing along the sequence (CSA), reusing across layers, and lowering storage precision (FP4). The cost is that low precision and reuse may lose information, so precise long-context retrieval must be evaluated. The \"Causal Encoder-Decoder\" also shows that the decoder-only skeleton is still seeing structural exploration in 2026.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- List the three stacked KV compression techniques.\n- Note the new feature of different activated parameter counts in prefill and decode.","L1-1.6.10":"Linear attention / recurrent-layer hybrids are another cost-cutting route: replace some layers with recurrent-style modules whose state size is fixed, so per-token compute and memory are O(1), and keep only a few global attention layers for precise retrieval.\n\n**How it works**\n- Modules such as linear attention and SSMs compress history into a fixed-size state, with no KV cache that grows with the sequence.\n- Representatives: Qwen3-Next → Qwen3.5 → Qwen3.8 use Gated DeltaNet and Gated Attention at 3:1, e.g. Qwen3.5-397B-A17B is 15 × (3 × DeltaNet + 1 × Attention); Kimi Linear → Kimi K3 use KDA + MLA, with K3 having 69 KDA layers + 24 Gated MLA layers; Ling 2.5 uses Lightning Attention + MLA; Nemotron 3 is mostly Mamba-2 + sparse MoE, keeping only a few GQA layers.\n- The Kimi Linear report: relative to a full-MLA baseline, the KV cache shrinks by up to 75%, and decoding throughput at 1M tokens improves by up to about 6×.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nA fixed-size state can't retrieve arbitrary positions precisely the way softmax attention can, so a few global layers are kept for \"exact lookup\", creating a division of labor. The cost is a more complex hybrid structure, and inference engines must support both kinds of cache.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Answering \"is it still a standard Transformer?\": the skeleton is, but attention layers are now a mix of local windows, sparse selection, KV compression and linear recurrent layers, driven by the cost of million-token contexts.\n- Cite 2 concrete hybrid ratios.","L1-1.7.1":"The KV cache is the core optimization of autoregressive decoding: store the Key and Value already computed for every layer and every past position, so the whole prefix isn't recomputed for each new token.\n\n**How it works**\n- Without a cache, step t reruns all layers over the first t−1 tokens, attention per step is O(t²), and generating n tokens costs O(n³) in total.\n- Key observation: because of the causal mask, position j's K and V in every layer depend only on tokens ≤ j, and later tokens never change them.\n- When a new token arrives: compute q, k, v for it only → append k and v to the cache → dot q with all cached K and take the weighted sum over V. Per-step attention drops to O(t).\n- With RoPE, the cache stores already-rotated K.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nIt trades memory for compute and is the default behavior of every inference engine; the cost is that the cache grows linearly with sequence length and concurrency, becoming the memory bottleneck at long context, which spawned designs such as GQA, MLA, sliding windows and KV compression.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Why not cache Q? A past position's query is used only once, to compute its own row, and is never needed again.\n- Be able to draw the comparison: \"no cache recomputes the whole t×t matrix vs with cache only the last row is computed\".\n- The KV cache depends on the causal structure; a bidirectional encoder can't be reused incrementally this way.","L1-1.7.2":"Hand-coding decoding with a KV cache is a frequent coding question. The point isn't just writing it but writing it correctly and being able to prove it's correct.\n\n**How it works**\nThree key implementation points:\n- Each layer caches **already-rotated** K and V (not Q); a new token's K gets RoPE applied before being appended.\n- Positions need an **offset**: a new token's position = cached length + 0..T−1; forgetting the offset misaligns the positional encoding.\n- The causal mask must be built for \"queries are the last T positions\", not as a square matrix starting from 0.\n\nVerify correctness with an **equivalence test**: for the same input, the per-step logits from incremental decoding with the cache must be `allclose` to the logits from a single full forward pass without the cache.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nKV cache bugs often throw no errors and just quietly degrade generation quality, so an equivalence assertion is the safety net. When debugging a Transformer with planted bugs, a common combination is: four assertions (shapes, each attention row sums to 1, causality, cache equivalence) + layer-by-layer comparison against a reference implementation + overfitting a single batch (loss should approach 0) + checking that the initial loss ≈ ln V (about 10.8 for V = 50,257).\n\n**Interview notes**\n- When asked \"how do you prove your cache implementation is correct?\", answer with the equivalence test, not \"run it and see if the output reads well\".\n- Under GQA, the cache holds n_kv heads, and K/V must be replicated per group to the corresponding query heads during computation.\n- An initial loss far above ln V usually means a problem with initialization or the softmax dimension.","L1-1.7.3":"Estimating KV cache memory is the most common calculation question in interviews, used to judge how long a context and how much concurrency a single GPU can handle.\n\n**How it works**\nFormula: `KV bytes = 2 × L × n_kv × d_head × T × B × bytes per element`\n- 2: one copy each for K and V; L: number of layers; n_kv: number of KV heads (fewer than query heads under GQA); d_head: per-head dimension; T: sequence length; B: number of concurrent sequences.\n\nUsing Llama-3-8B as an example (32 layers, 8 KV heads, d_head=128, FP16):\n- Per token: 2×32×8×128×2 bytes = 128 KiB\n- About 1 GiB at 8k context, about 16 GiB at 128k context\n- With MHA instead (32 KV heads), it becomes 512 KiB per token, 4× GQA.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nPer-token cache can differ by more than an order of magnitude across architectures: among models of roughly 30B, Muse Glimmer 30B uses about 52 KiB per token while Gemma 4 31B uses about 840 KiB per token (BF16, as converted in a blog post). When comparing numbers from different sources, first check the basis: whether local-window layers are included, at what precision, and whether offloadable parts are counted.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Be able to compute Llama-3-8B's 128 KiB per token in your head and scale it to any length.\n- Sliding-window layers only cache tokens within the window; don't simply compute over the full length.\n- Related exercise: read `config.json` to automatically compute per-token cache and maximum concurrency, and compare the error against measurements.","L1-1.7.4":"In long-context scenarios, the main consumer of GPU memory shifts from model weights to the KV cache, which directly determines how many requests a single GPU can serve and the unit cost.\n\n**How it works**\n- An 8B model's FP16 weights are about 16 GB; at Llama-3-8B's 128 KiB per token, the KV cache for one 128k sequence is about 16 GiB — almost as large as the entire model's weights.\n- The KV cache scales with concurrency: total cache = context length per request × cache per token × concurrency. Whatever memory remains after the weights is basically eaten by the cache.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\n- How many requests can be served at once is largely determined by \"context length × cache per token\" — that is the cost model.\n- This is exactly why GQA / MLA / sliding windows / KV compression / low-precision KV exist: they compress the cache, not the weights.\n- Quantizing weights solves only half the problem; with long context and high concurrency, prioritize KV-side optimizations and memory management such as PagedAttention.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- One-line takeaway: at long context the memory bottleneck is the cache, not the weights.\n- When asked \"why are long-context APIs expensive?\", connect cache usage, reduced concurrency and unit cost.\n- Be able to estimate: given 80 GB of memory and a given context length, the maximum number of concurrent sequences.","L1-1.7.5":"LLM inference has two phases of completely different character, prefill and decode. Understanding them is the basis for latency optimization and for explaining API pricing.\n\n**How it works**\n- **Prefill**: processes the whole prompt in parallel in one go and builds the KV cache. It's large matrix multiplication — compute-bound, eating FLOPs — and determines **TTFT** (time to first token).\n- **Decode**: generates token by token; each step computes only one row but has to read all weights and the KV cache, so it's memory-bandwidth-bound and throughput is limited by bandwidth; it corresponds to **TPOT** (time per output token) and tokens/s.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\n- This explains \"input is cheap, output is expensive\": input can be parallelized, output must be serial. APIs typically price output at several times input; flagship models in September 2026 are mostly around 5×.\n- The two phases have different bottlenecks and thus different optimizations: prefill relies on compute and efficient kernels; decode relies on larger batches, quantization and speculative decoding to amortize bandwidth.\n- A long prompt's prefill squeezed into the same scheduling step as decode inflates decode latency, hence schemes such as chunked prefill and prefill-decode (PD) disaggregation.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Which phase determines TTFT and which TPOT, and how to optimize each.\n- Why small-batch decode is almost always bandwidth-bound.\n- Use the two phases to explain the physical reason output tokens are priced higher.","L1-1.7.6":"The decode bandwidth lower bound is the first yardstick for diagnosing decode latency: first compute \"the theoretical best case\", then see how far the measurement is from it.\n\n**How it works**\n- Each decode step must read all the weights at least once, plus the KV cache of every sequence in the batch. Lower bound = bytes that must be read ÷ peak memory bandwidth.\n- Bandwidth utilization = bytes that must be read per step ÷ (step time × peak bandwidth).\n- Example: Llama-3-8B's BF16 weights are 16.06 GB and an H100 SXM has 3.35 TB/s, so each token takes at least 4.8 ms, capping out at about 209 tok/s. At 32k context with GQA-8, each sequence's KV is 4 GiB: the lower bound is about 6.1 ms at B=1, about 15.1 ms at B=8 and about 45.8 ms at B=32, with KV's share of bytes read rising from 21% to 90%.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\n- MQA / GQA only shrink the KV term and do nothing for the weights term: shrinking KV another 8× cuts the B=8 lower bound from 15.1 ms to 6.1 ms, but B=1 only goes from 6.1 to about 5.0 ms.\n- The H100's dense BF16 compute-to-bandwidth ratio is about 295 FLOPs/byte, while decode does only about B multiply-adds per byte of weights, so the batch has to reach the hundreds before decode becomes compute-bound.\n- Low utilization points to overhead (TP all-reduce, kernel launches, CPU scheduling, prefill preemption); if utilization is already high, the only option is to read fewer bytes (quantization, larger batches, speculative decoding).\n\n**Interview notes**\n- The standard answer to \"latency is still high even with GQA\": measure before guessing, and compare against the bandwidth lower bound.\n- At long context, the attention kernel must parallelize along the KV length (Flash-Decoding).","L1-1.7.7":"Prompt caching persists the KV cache across requests: when multiple requests share an identical prefix (system prompt, tool definitions, long documents), from the second request onward the prefix's KV is reused directly, skipping prefill.\n\n**How it works**\n- **Only prefixes can be reused**: the KV at position j depends on every token before it, so changing any token in the prefix invalidates all cache after it.\n- So put stable content first and volatile content later.\n- Cache reads are priced far below normal input; e.g. Anthropic's docs list cache reads at 10% of the base input price (2.5% for Fable 5.1, 5% for Opus 5.5).\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\n- Agents resend the accumulated context at every step; cache hits bill most of that input at the cache price while also lowering TTFT.\n- Common cost incidents: a second-precision timestamp in the system prompt, or a tool list in a different order each time, drops the hit rate to zero.\n- A non-obvious trap: Anthropic renders the effort value into the prompt, so switching the top-level effort between requests invalidates earlier cache breakpoints; Fable 5.1, Opus 5.5, Opus 5 and Sonnet 5.5 offer a beta per-message effort switch that changes the level mid-conversation without breaking the cache.\n- Compressing history also invalidates subsequent cache, so weigh the tokens saved against the hits lost.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Why only prefixes can be reused (the causal structure).\n- List at least two engineering mistakes that invalidate the cache.\n- How to verify hits using the cache fields in usage.","L1-1.8.1":"Long context is not as simple as \"make the window bigger\". As of September 2026, most flagship windows are on the order of 1M tokens, but \"supports 1M\" and \"still works well and affordably at 1M\" are different things. The challenge can be analyzed in three layers.\n\n**How it works**\n- **Position layer**: can the model recognize positions that far away? Training only saw lengths ≤ N, so at inference RoPE angles fall into an unseen range. Remedies: raise the RoPE base, position interpolation, NTK-aware scaling, YaRN, and more fundamentally continued training on long sequences; some hybrid architectures use no positional encoding in global layers.\n- **Compute and memory layer**: can it be computed, can it be stored? O(n²) attention compute and a KV cache that grows linearly with length, addressed by FlashAttention, GQA/MLA, sliding windows, sparse attention, KV compression and so on.\n- **Effective utilization layer**: once it's in there, does the model actually use it? Lost in the middle and context rot both show that utilization declines with length.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nThis three-layer framework gives the answer structure: the first two layers make long context \"run\", the third decides whether it's \"worth using\". Candidates from research tend to cover only the first two, while in engineering the third layer — plus cost and latency — deserves the most emphasis.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- When asked \"how do you support 1M context?\", organize the answer in three layers.\n- Stress that long context is a capability ceiling, not the default, and be able to judge when to switch to retrieval.","L1-1.8.2":"The compute and memory layer answers \"can long context be computed and stored?\"; each technique saves on attention compute, the KV cache or repeated prefill.\n\n**How it works**\n- **FlashAttention**: saves attention intermediate memory and constant factors; exact results, no quality cost.\n- **GQA / MLA**: compress the KV cache by heads or by rank, at the cost of slight quality loss or implementation complexity.\n- **Sliding window + a few global layers**: saves compute and cache in local layers, with long-range information relayed by global layers.\n- **Sparse selection (DSA)**: saves global-layer compute, at the risk of the indexer missing tokens.\n- **Compressing KV along the sequence (CSA / HCA)**: saves both compute and cache; fine-grained information may be lost.\n- **Linear attention / SSM hybrids**: most layers become O(1) state, with precise retrieval relying on the remaining attention layers.\n- **Low-precision KV (FP8 / FP4)**: shrinks cache size at the cost of numeric error.\n- **Prompt caching**: saves prefill on repeated prefixes; only works for stable prefixes.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nApart from FlashAttention, which is \"free\", all of these trade quality, implementation complexity or information loss for efficiency. When choosing, look at whether the bottleneck is compute (long prefill) or cache (high concurrency), and verify on long-context retrieval evaluations that no key capability is lost.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Say what each technique saves and what it costs, rather than just listing names.\n- Distinguish \"exact\" optimizations (FlashAttention) from \"approximate\" ones (sliding windows, sparsity, compression).","L1-1.8.3":"Lost in the middle means that when relevant information is in the middle of the context, the model uses it markedly less than when it's at the beginning or end (Liu et al., 2023).\n\n**How it works**\nThe experiment places the key document or fact at different positions in a long context and measures answer accuracy; the result is U-shaped: high at both ends, low in the middle. Common explanations include primacy and recency positional biases in attention, early tokens soaking up much of the attention (attention sink), and softmax attention dilution over long sequences.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\n- Directly relevant to RAG and agents: the order in which retrieval results are concatenated, and where early instructions sit in a long conversation, affect whether the model can use the information.\n- Engineering remedies: trim the context, remove conflicts and distractors, put key information and key constraints at the beginning and end, and restate key instructions at the end when needed.\n- It complements context rot: lost in the middle is about positional effects, context rot about overall degradation from length and distractors. It's also one context-level cause of hallucination.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Describe the U-shaped curve and how to design a verification experiment: insert the key fact at positions such as 10%, 30%, 50%, 70% and 90%, add semantically similar distractor facts, and plot an accuracy heatmap.\n- Give at least two context-engineering mitigations.\n- Explain that fitting in the window doesn't mean being used.","L1-1.8.4":"Context rot means that even when the task itself is simple (retrieval, repetition), model reliability keeps declining as input gets longer. It shows there's a big gap between \"fits in the window\" and \"can be used reliably\".\n\n**How it works**\n- Chroma's July 2025 technical report tested 18 models and found that the longer the input, the lower the reliability; the decline is steeper the lower the semantic similarity between question and \"needle\" and the more distractors there are.\n- Official data corroborates this: DeepSeek-V4's long-context retrieval (MRCR 8-needle) accuracy stays above 0.82 up to 256K but drops to 0.59 at 1M.\n- Simple needle-in-a-haystack (NIAH) tests are saturated and misleading: finding a sentence that sticks out from the surrounding text is easy, while real tasks require reasoning that synthesizes information from multiple places.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nIt's especially damaging for agents: tool outputs keep piling up noise and distractors, degrading subsequent decisions. Mitigations include compressing / summarizing old tool outputs, externalizing state to files or memory, and isolating context with sub-agents. It's also the main argument against \"a big enough window means no need for RAG / context engineering\".\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Distinguish context rot (overall degradation from length and distractors) from lost in the middle (positional effects).\n- Explain why a high NIAH score doesn't mean long context works well; use multi-needle evaluations with distractors that require synthesis instead.\n- Give concrete mitigations for agent scenarios.","L1-1.8.5":"Long context and retrieval (RAG) are not substitutes. The core engineering takeaway: long context is a capability ceiling, not the default way to use the model.\n\n**How it works**\nFirst, the cost and latency reality of long context:\n- Input is billed by volume, and agents resend the accumulated context every step; without caching, the total input cost of an N-step task is on the order of O(N²).\n- Prefill time grows with input length, and time to first token for million-token inputs can reach tens of seconds or more.\n- Some vendors charge a higher unit price for inputs beyond a certain length.\n\nThen choose by scenario:\n- **Fine to put directly into context**: a single long document needing holistic understanding, codebase-level refactoring; one-off use where latency and cost don't matter much; densely cross-referenced information where chunking would break the structure; stable prefixes that can hit the cache.\n- **Better served by retrieval / compression / divide-and-conquer**: Q&A over large knowledge bases (most content is irrelevant to the question); high QPS and latency sensitivity; information that can be chunked independently and needs source citation and access control; frequently changing content.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nLong context removes chunking, indexing and the risk of retrieval misses, but pays in cost, latency and declining effective utilization (context rot); retrieval is cheaper, citable and supports access control, but anything retrieval misses, the model never sees.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- When asked \"with 1M context, do we still need RAG?\", answer with the four comparisons above.\n- Mention hybrid approaches: narrow the scope with retrieval, then provide ample context, and use prompt caching.","L1-1.8.6":"The context capacity budget answers: how much can an agent actually fit in? The answer has two layers, hard limits and soft limits, and usable input must first subtract the reserves for output and thinking.\n\n**How it works**\n- **Hard limits**: the model's window, the API's maximum input / output length, and concurrency as determined by KV memory.\n- **Soft limits**: effective utilization (dilution, positional bias, distractors), and latency and cost budgets.\n- Budget formula: `usable input ≈ window − max_output − thinking reserve − safety margin`; already used = system prompt + tool schemas + history + this turn's tool outputs. With too small a reserve, thinking and output get truncated.\n- Cumulative cost: without caching, an N-step agent's cumulative input ≈ `N·S + Δ·N(N+1)/2` (S is the fixed prefix, Δ the per-step addition). With S=10k, Δ=2k and N=30, that's about 1.2M tokens — quadratic growth.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nAttention's limitations in multi-turn agents include dilution, positional bias, attention sinks, tool-output noise, instruction drift, no persistent state, and thinking tokens sharing the window with output. So don't fill the window. Mitigations: compress old tool outputs when a threshold is reached, externalize state, isolate with sub-agents, restate key constraints at the end, and keep the prefix stable to hit the cache.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Be able to write the usable-input budget formula and the quadratic cost formula and plug in numbers.\n- Don't crudely cut the beginning when trimming context: the attention sink phenomenon shows early tokens matter a lot for behavior.\n- Compressing history breaks the prompt cache, so weigh the trade-off.","L1-1.9.1":"MoE (Mixture of Experts) is an architecture that greatly expands model capacity without significantly increasing per-token compute.\n\n**How it works**\nReplace one FFN in each block with N parallel FFNs (experts) and add a router (gate). For each token, the router picks only the top-k experts to run:\n- `g = softmax(TopK(W_r · x))`\n- `y = Σ_{i∈TopK} g_i · E_i(x)`\n\nThe attention part usually stays unchanged and is shared by all tokens. Typical configurations: Mixtral 8x7B picks 2 of 8; DeepSeek-V3 has 1 shared expert + 256 routed experts per layer, picking 8; gpt-oss-120b has 128 experts per layer, picking top-4; Kimi K3 picks 16 of 896.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nThe FFN holds most of the model's stored knowledge, and knowledge is used sparsely (French grammar is irrelevant when processing Python code). MoE decouples capacity from compute: under the same training and inference compute budget, it usually beats a dense model with the same compute. The price is a set of engineering problems: load balancing, memory footprint, batching efficiency and training stability.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Be able to write the routing formula and explain that MoE replaces the FFN, not attention.\n- Be able to cite the expert configurations of two or three mainstream MoE models.\n- Dense → MoE is the general migration direction for large models, because it decouples capacity from compute.","L1-1.9.2":"MoE models have two parameter counts: total parameters and active parameters. Confusing the two is a common interview mistake.\n\n**How it works**\n- **Total parameters**: the sum over all experts; determines how much knowledge the model \"holds\" and how much memory it needs.\n- **Active parameters**: the parameters a single token actually passes through; determines per-token compute and speed.\n\nTypical examples:\n- Mixtral 8x7B: ~47B / 13B\n- DeepSeek-V3: 671B / 37B\n- gpt-oss-120b: ~117B / 5.1B\n- DeepSeek-V4-Pro: 1.6T / 49B\n- Kimi K3: 2.8T / 104B\n\nTrend: open flagship models have reached trillion-scale total parameters, with active ratios mostly at 3%–6% (hybrid architectures such as Nemotron 3 are around 10%).\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\n- Use active parameters to estimate inference speed and FLOPs; use total parameters to estimate deployment memory. A trillion-parameter model with only tens of billions active still needs hundreds of GB or even TB-scale GPU memory to deploy.\n- That is why vendors are quantizing expert weights to 4-bit (FP4 / MXFP4).\n- When comparing with dense models, state which count you mean; never compare active parameters directly against a dense model's total parameters.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- \"Why isn't Mixtral 8x7B 56B?\": attention and other parts are shared; it is not 8 full copies of the model.\n- Given total parameters and precision, be able to estimate weight memory.","L1-1.9.3":"Load balancing is the top challenge in MoE training: without constraints, the router sends most tokens to a few experts, a rich-get-richer \"routing collapse\" that leaves the other experts untrained.\n\n**How it works**\n- **Auxiliary load-balancing loss** (standard since Switch Transformer): add a term to the training objective that penalizes uneven expert usage. The downside is that this loss conflicts with the language modeling objective; weighting it too heavily hurts the main task.\n- **DeepSeek-V3's auxiliary-loss-free approach**: no loss term; instead, add a dynamically adjusted bias to each expert's routing score — lower it when the expert is overloaded, raise it when underloaded — achieving balance without interfering with the main objective's gradients.\n- Other techniques: a capacity limit (capacity factor; excess tokens overflow or are dropped), routing noise, expert dropout. Qwen3 MoE uses a global-batch load-balancing loss.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nLoad imbalance not only wastes capacity but also overloads some devices in distributed deployments, dragging down overall throughput. The auxiliary loss is simple but conflicts with the main objective; aux-loss-free is cleaner, but the bias update rate also needs tuning.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Explain the causes and consequences of routing collapse.\n- Contrast the auxiliary loss with aux-loss-free dynamic bias: one changes the gradients, the other only changes routing choices.\n- The scope of balancing statistics (per-batch vs. global-batch) affects how specialized experts become.","L1-1.9.4":"Shared experts are a design introduced by DeepSeekMoE: alongside the routed experts, a few experts are always active and dedicated to the general knowledge every kind of token needs.\n\n**How it works**\n- Each token's output = the shared experts' output + the weighted output of the top-k routed experts the router selects.\n- Shared experts process every token and bypass the router.\n- Typical configurations: DeepSeek-V3 has 1 shared expert + 256 routed experts per layer, picking 8; DeepSeek-V4-Pro has 1 shared + 384 routed per layer, picking 6; Qwen3.5-397B-A17B uses 10 routed experts + 1 shared expert per token.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\n- Without shared experts, every routed expert has to learn general knowledge (grammar, common token patterns) on its own, creating parameter redundancy. Shared experts centralize the general part so routed experts only learn what differentiates them, improving parameter efficiency and specialization.\n- They are not mandatory: Qwen3 MoE (8 of 128) has no shared experts. Whether to use them is an architectural trade-off settled by experiments.\n- Shared experts are always active, so they count toward active parameters and increase per-token compute.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Make clear that shared experts address redundancy.\n- Name representative models with and without shared experts.\n- Shared and routed experts call for different placement strategies at deployment (the former is accessed by every token).","L1-1.9.5":"MoE is often assumed to be \"cheap,\" but it saves compute, not memory. Deploying MoE carries a set of costs that dense models don't have.\n\n**How it works**\n- **No memory savings**: all experts must stay resident in GPU memory. A trillion-parameter model with only tens of billions active still needs hundreds of GB or even TB-scale memory. This is why DeepSeek-V4, Kimi K3 and gpt-oss store expert weights in 4-bit (FP4 / MXFP4).\n- **Lower batching efficiency**: tokens in the same batch go to different experts, so what would be large dense matrix multiplications get split into many small pieces, lowering GPU utilization.\n- **Distributed complexity**: expert parallelism is needed to decide how experts are placed across devices; tokens must be dispatched and gathered across devices (all-to-all communication), and load imbalance creates hot-spot devices.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nMoE shines in high-concurrency, large-cluster settings: less compute, higher throughput. But for single-GPU, small-batch, memory-constrained settings (such as local deployment), a dense model with the same memory footprint may be the better deal. Model selection must weigh memory budget, concurrency and communication bandwidth together.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- One-liner: MoE saves FLOPs, not memory.\n- Explain why fragmented batches lower GPU utilization.\n- Why MoE is especially suited to low-bit quantization: the weights are large, yet each token reads only a small fraction.","L1-1.9.6":"MoE is harder to tune than dense models in both training and fine-tuning, and the root cause is discrete routing.\n\n**How it works**\n- **Non-differentiable**: top-k selection is a discrete operation; usually gradients flow back only through the gate weights of the selected experts, and unselected experts get no signal from that token.\n- **Routing oscillation**: early in training the router's choices tend to swing back and forth — the same kind of token goes to expert A one moment and expert B the next — making it hard for experts to specialize stably and making training curves less stable.\n- **Fine-tuning is trickier**: applying LoRA to MoE first requires deciding which modules to adapt — attention only? The router? All experts? With small fine-tuning datasets, the data may be routed to only a few experts, leaving the rest barely trained.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\n- Tuning only attention is the most stable but may do little for knowledge injection; tuning experts can inject domain knowledge but costs more parameters and memory, with uneven coverage; tuning the router may break the load balance established during pre-training.\n- In practice, monitor the expert load distribution before and after fine-tuning to catch fine-tuning-induced routing collapse.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Explain why top-k routing causes gradient and stability problems.\n- When asked \"how do you apply LoRA to MoE,\" list the module choices and their pros and cons.\n- Mention monitoring the routing distribution as a health metric for fine-tuning.","L1-1.9.7":"A common misconception is that \"each expert corresponds to a domain,\" e.g. a math expert or a code expert. That is not what happens.\n\n**How it works**\n- Empirical analysis (e.g. the Mixtral paper) shows routing patterns correlate more with **syntactic / token-level features** — punctuation, indentation, particular parts of speech — than with topic domains in the human sense.\n- The division of labor among experts emerges spontaneously during training, shaped by load-balancing constraints and optimization dynamics, with no semantics assigned by hand, so it is usually not clearly interpretable.\n- Routing happens per token and per layer: adjacent tokens in the same sentence may go to completely different experts, and experts in different layers have no correspondence with each other.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\n- This shapes engineering decisions: you can't expect to prune for a domain by \"loading only the code experts,\" and when doing small-data domain fine-tuning on MoE you can't assume only \"the experts for that domain\" need updating.\n- There is tension between expert specialization and load balancing: the stronger the balancing constraint, the harder it is for experts to develop distinct roles.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Proactively correct the \"expert = domain\" misconception and cite the Mixtral paper's analysis.\n- Explain that routing is per token and per layer, not per document.\n- You can add: the shared-expert design exists precisely to let routed experts focus on differentiated features.","L1-1.10.1":"Greedy and beam search are two deterministic decoding methods that \"look for high-probability sequences.\" A decoding strategy doesn't change the model; it only decides how to pick a token from the vocabulary distribution at each step.\n\n**How it works**\n- **Greedy**: take the highest-probability token at each step. Deterministic and fast, but a local optimum isn't a global optimum; it easily falls into repetition loops and produces monotonous text.\n- **Beam search**: keep the B candidate sequences with the highest cumulative probability, expand them each step, prune back to B, and finally choose the highest-scoring one. Length normalization is usually needed; otherwise it favors short sequences.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nBeam search works for tasks where \"the output is largely determined by the input,\" such as translation and speech recognition, but is almost never used for open-ended generation, for three reasons:\n- **Likelihood trap**: high-likelihood text tends to be bland and repetitive; human text is not the most probable text;\n- **Cost**: B times the compute and KV cache;\n- **Hard to stream**: which sequence wins isn't known until the end, so outputting while generating is awkward.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Why LLM chat almost always uses sampling rather than beam search.\n- Why beam search needs length normalization.\n- Greedy is equivalent to sampling with temperature → 0.","L1-1.10.2":"Temperature is the most basic sampling knob. It controls how sharp the output distribution is, trading off determinism against diversity.\n\n**How it works**\nDivide the logits by T before softmax: `p_i = exp(z_i / T) / Σ_j exp(z_j / T)`\n- T < 1: sharper distribution; high-probability tokens dominate more and output is more deterministic;\n- T > 1: flatter distribution; low-probability tokens get more chances and output is more random;\n- T → 0: equivalent to greedy.\n\nKey property: temperature **does not change the probability ranking of tokens**, only the gaps between them.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\n- Choose by scenario: low temperature (0–0.3) for extraction, classification and tool-call arguments; 0.6–0.8 for chat; 0.9–1.2 for creative writing; higher temperature with multiple samples when you need diverse candidates.\n- High temperature amplifies the probability of junk long-tail tokens, so it is usually paired with top-p or min-p; the order of temperature and truncation also affects results.\n- High temperature and loose top-p raise the chance of picking a wrong token, one of the sampling-level causes of hallucination.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Write the formula and explain that temperature doesn't change the ranking.\n- Explain why temperature = 0 isn't fully deterministic in practice (GPU floating-point ordering and batch composition).\n- In implementations, temperature = 0 needs a separate argmax branch to avoid division by zero.","L1-1.10.3":"Top-k and top-p are two forms of truncated sampling: first cut off the unreliable long-tail tokens, then sample from the remaining candidates by probability.\n\n**How it works**\n- **Top-k**: keep only the k highest-probability tokens, renormalize, then sample.\n- **Top-p (nucleus sampling)**: accumulate tokens in descending order of probability and keep the **smallest set** whose cumulative probability first reaches p. p is typically 0.9–0.95.\n\nExample: with probabilities [0.607, 0.223, 0.135, 0.030, 0.004], top-p=0.5 keeps only the first (0.607 ≥ 0.5), and top-p=0.7 keeps the first two (0.607+0.223 ≥ 0.7).\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\n- Top-k's problem is that k is fixed: when the distribution is sharp (the model is confident), k=50 lets in a pile of junk tokens; when it is flat, k=50 may cut off reasonable candidates.\n- Top-p's candidate count adapts to the distribution's shape — only one or two when confident, many when uncertain — which is why it has been the most common default for the past few years.\n- Top-p's weakness is high temperature: once the distribution is flattened, reaching p takes many tokens and the long tail floods in, which motivated min-p.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Be able to hand-code top-p: sort descending, cumsum, searchsorted, keep at least 1.\n- Contrast the adaptivity of top-k and top-p.\n- Explain that the two can be stacked, and the ordering issue with temperature.","L1-1.10.4":"Min-p is a truncated sampling method whose threshold scales with model confidence (Nguyen et al., 2024), designed to keep blocking junk long-tail tokens even at high temperature.\n\n**How it works**\nKeep every token satisfying `p_i ≥ min_p × p_max`, where p_max is the current highest token probability.\n- When the model is confident (large p_max), the threshold is high and only a few candidates remain;\n- When the model is uncertain (small p_max), the threshold drops accordingly, allowing more candidates.\n\nExample: with probabilities [0.607, 0.223, 0.135, 0.030, 0.004] and min_p=0.3, the threshold is about 0.182, keeping the first two tokens.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\n- Difference from top-p: top-p truncates by cumulative probability, so once temperature flattens the distribution it admits many long-tail tokens; min-p uses a threshold relative to the top probability and keeps excluding clearly unreliable tokens, making it more robust for high-temperature creative generation.\n- Widely used in open-source inference stacks: llama.cpp, various local inference front-ends and Hugging Face Transformers all support it; llama.cpp defaults to min-p 0.05.\n- It is only available when self-hosting open models; newer closed APIs have withdrawn sampling parameters.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Write the min-p criterion and explain \"the threshold scales with confidence.\"\n- Explain why min-p beats top-p at high temperature.\n- In implementations, the order of temperature and truncation affects how min-p behaves.","L1-1.10.5":"Repetition penalties are a family of methods that lower the probability of \"tokens that have already appeared,\" suppressing parroting and loops in generation.\n\n**How it works**\n- **Repetition penalty**: originates from the CTRL paper. The common implementation (e.g. Hugging Face): for tokens already seen, divide positive logits by θ and multiply negative logits by θ (θ > 1); either way the token becomes less likely. This is multiplicative.\n- **Frequency penalty** (OpenAI style, additive): subtract an amount proportional to how many times the token has appeared — the more it appears, the bigger the deduction.\n- **Presence penalty** (additive): subtract a constant once the token has appeared at all, regardless of count, encouraging new topics.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\n- Penalties can relieve repetition loops under greedy or low temperature and make long text more natural.\n- The side effects are significant: an overly strong penalty makes the model avoid content that must repeat, such as variable names in code, JSON keys and proper nouns, leading to code that won't run or rewritten JSON keys. So they should usually be turned off for structured output.\n- In processing order, penalties generally act on the raw logits before temperature and truncation.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Be able to write the positive and negative branches of the multiplicative repetition penalty and explain why negative logits are multiplied rather than divided.\n- Distinguish frequency from presence.\n- Give examples of scenarios where the repetition penalty must be off.","L1-1.10.6":"Typical sampling and logit bias are two less-discussed but useful decoding controls: the former is another truncation approach, the latter a manual intervention on specific tokens.\n\n**How it works**\n- **Typical sampling**: instead of truncating by probability, keep tokens whose information content (`−log p_i`) is close to the conditional entropy of the current distribution. The intuition is that the \"surprise\" of each word in human text is usually near average; words that are too predictable or too surprising are atypical. It can exclude both very-high-probability but vacuous tokens and very-low-probability noise tokens. llama.cpp's default sampler chain also includes a typical-p step.\n- **Logit bias**: add a positive or negative value to the logits of specified tokens before sampling, manually raising or lowering their probability; adding a very large negative value effectively bans the token.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\n- Typical sampling helps somewhat with reducing repetitive and bland output, but it is less widespread than top-p, with less tuning experience available.\n- Logit bias suits banning certain words or forcing classification labels to come from a few tokens, but it operates on tokens, not words; a word may be split into several tokens and slip through. More complex format constraints should use constrained decoding.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Explain how typical sampling's \"information content close to entropy\" idea differs from top-p.\n- Give typical uses of logit bias and the limitations the tokenizer introduces.","L1-1.10.7":"Constrained decoding zeroes out the probability of invalid tokens at every step, forcing the output to satisfy a given JSON Schema or grammar. It is the underlying mechanism of structured outputs.\n\n**How it works**\n- Compile the JSON Schema, regex or context-free grammar into a state machine or grammar parser.\n- At each step, based on the prefix generated so far, compute which tokens keep the output valid, producing a token mask.\n- Set the logits of invalid tokens to negative infinity (zero probability), then sample with the usual temperature / truncation strategy.\n- When generation finishes, the output is guaranteed syntactically valid, with no post-hoc parse-and-retry needed.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\n- For tool-call arguments, information extraction, SQL generation and similar tasks, it turns \"JSON parse rate\" into a syntax-level guarantee, eliminating retry and repair logic.\n- Limitations: it guarantees only valid format, not correct content; overly strict constraints can force the model onto low-probability paths and hurt quality; mask computation adds overhead, and misalignment between tokenizer token boundaries and grammar boundaries makes implementation harder.\n- Pair it with low temperature and turn off the repetition penalty, since JSON keys need to repeat.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Explain which step of decoding it acts on (logit masking).\n- Distinguish \"correct format\" from \"correct semantics.\"\n- Advantage over prompting for JSON: it goes from \"valid with high probability\" to a structural guarantee.","L1-1.10.8":"Sampling processing order is the sequence in which temperature, top-k, top-p, min-p and other steps are applied. Different orders produce different candidate sets from the same parameters, and mainstream implementations don't agree.\n\n**How it works**\n- **Temperature first, then truncation**: current Hugging Face Transformers adds temperature processing first in `_get_logits_processor`, then top-k, top-p and min-p; the min-p paper's implementation also applies temperature scaling first and then computes the threshold on the scaled distribution, calling this the more common approach.\n- **Truncation first, then temperature**: first decide which tokens qualify on the raw distribution, then use temperature to adjust randomness among them, so high temperature can't let junk tokens into the candidate set. llama.cpp's current default sampler chain works this way: penalties → DRY → top-n-sigma → top-k → typical-p → top-p → min-p → XTC → temperature, with defaults temp 0.8, top-k 40, top-p 0.95, min-p 0.05.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\n- The difference is largest at high temperature: with temperature first, the distribution is flattened and top-p admits more long-tail tokens; with truncation first, the candidate set is unaffected by temperature.\n- When migrating parameters between inference stacks, the same numbers may behave differently; evaluation and production need to use the same implementation.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- When writing sampling code in an interview, proactively say \"order affects the result; I'm following XX's convention here.\"\n- Be able to cite the different approaches of Transformers and llama.cpp.\n- Repetition penalties usually act first, on the raw logits.","L1-1.10.9":"Scenario-based decoding configuration turns decoding principles into engineering practice: different tasks need different amounts of determinism and diversity, so parameters should differ too.\n\n**How it works**\n- **Extraction, classification, tool-call arguments, SQL / code generation**: low temperature (0–0.3), constrained decoding for structured output; the goal is determinism and reproducibility.\n- **Chat, general Q&A**: temperature 0.6–0.8 + top-p 0.9–0.95, natural without being rigid.\n- **Creative writing, brainstorming**: temperature 0.9–1.2 + top-p or min-p, aiming for diversity.\n- **When you need multiple distinct candidates** (self-consistency, synthetic data): sample multiple times at higher temperature; diversity itself is the goal.\n- **Evaluation**: fix the temperature (often 0) and run multiple times, reporting the variance, to keep results comparable.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\n\"temperature = 0\" does not mean fully deterministic: on GPUs, floating-point operation order depends on batch composition, so logits can differ very slightly; when two tokens have nearly equal probability, the argmax can flip and the entire continuation diverges. Add provider model updates and different hardware backends, and strict reproducibility requires self-hosting and pinning everything. Also, reasoning models' model cards often give specifically recommended sampling parameters — for example, Qwen3 thinking mode explicitly says not to use greedy decoding.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Be able to recommend parameters by scenario and justify them.\n- Explain why temperature = 0 can still be non-deterministic.\n- Report variance in evaluations, not single-run results.","L1-1.10.10":"An important 2026 trend: the most advanced closed API models are withdrawing sampling knobs, and developers can no longer freely tune temperature and truncation parameters.\n\n**How it works**\n- Anthropic's migration docs are explicit: on newer Claude models, `temperature`, `top_p` and `top_k` may only be omitted or left at default values; other values are rejected, and prompting is recommended to steer behavior instead. Assistant prefill (pre-filling the start of the response) is also no longer accepted.\n- The change started with Opus 4.7 (2026-04-16), which began rejecting non-default sampling parameters.\n- The rationale: reasoning models' behavior is controlled more by thinking and effort, and arbitrarily changing sampling parameters can easily degrade reasoning quality.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\n- Knowledge of decoding strategies is now mainly applied to self-hosted open models, fine-tuning evaluation and understanding the fundamentals.\n- With closed APIs, the control levers have moved to effort levels, prompting (stating goals, constraints and acceptance criteria clearly) and structured outputs.\n- When migrating old code, check whether it passes non-default sampling parameters or relies on prefill to control format; otherwise requests will be rejected. Format control previously done via prefill should switch to structured outputs.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- When asked \"how do you make Claude's output more stable,\" don't just say \"lower the temperature\"; talk about effort, prompting and structured outputs.\n- Explain why reasoning models aren't suited to arbitrary sampling-parameter changes.","L1-1.10.11":"Speculative decoding is a decoding acceleration technique that leaves the output distribution unchanged: a cheap draft model guesses first, and the large model verifies the guesses in a single batch.\n\n**How it works**\n1. A small draft model guesses several tokens in a row;\n2. The large model computes the distributions at those positions in parallel in one forward pass;\n3. Modified rejection sampling decides how many to accept, and at the first rejected position a token is resampled from a corrected distribution.\n\nKey property: **the output distribution is exactly the same as sampling from the large model alone**, with no retraining or architectural change. The original paper reports 2–3x speedups on T5-XXL, and concurrent DeepMind work reports 2–2.5x on the 70B Chinchilla.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\n- Why it works: the decode phase is memory-bandwidth-bound, so verifying k tokens costs about the same as generating 1; reading the weights once yields multiple tokens.\n- The speedup depends on the draft model's acceptance rate: the closer the draft is to the large model, the faster; with a low acceptance rate it wastes compute.\n- At large batch sizes the GPU is already close to compute-bound, so the gain from extra verification shrinks.\n- Keeping the distribution unchanged requires applying the same temperature / top-p processing to the target distribution during verification; under greedy decoding it reduces to comparing the argmax position by position.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Explain why the output distribution is unchanged (rejection sampling).\n- Explain the speedup via decode being bandwidth-bound.\n- The speedup is determined by the acceptance rate.","L1-1.10.12":"MTP (Multi-Token Prediction) trains the model to additionally predict several subsequent tokens at each position; at inference, the extra prediction heads can serve as the model's built-in draft.\n\n**How it works**\n- Training: in addition to standard next-token prediction, add several prediction heads (or lightweight modules) that predict the 2nd, 3rd, ... subsequent tokens as auxiliary training objectives. This provides a denser training signal.\n- Inference: the extra heads guess the next few tokens in one go, and the main model verifies them as in speculative decoding, so no separate draft model needs to be maintained.\n- Examples: the DeepSeek-V4 technical report sets MTP depth to 1; Raschka's survey notes that Step 3.5 Flash uses MTP-3 in both training and inference.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\n- Compared with an external draft model, MTP heads share representations with the main model, so acceptance rates are often higher and deployment is simpler (no need to align the tokenizer and version of two models).\n- The cost is extra parameters and compute during training; the greater the depth, the harder it is to guess far-off tokens, and acceptance rates decline.\n- Together with speculative decoding, it belongs to the techniques that \"only change how fast the same distribution is computed,\" not the sampling strategy itself.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Explain that MTP is both a training objective and an inference acceleration method.\n- Compare MTP with speculative decoding using a separate draft model.\n- Be able to name models that use MTP.","L1-1.11.1":"Perplexity (PPL) is the most basic intrinsic metric for language models, measuring how \"perplexed\" a model is when predicting a piece of text.\n\n**How it works**\nFormula: `PPL = exp( −(1/N) Σ log p(x_i | x_<i) )`, i.e. the exponential of the average per-token cross-entropy.\n- Intuitively it is the **effective branching factor**: PPL = 20 means the model's uncertainty at each position is equivalent to choosing among 20 equally likely options.\n- With uniform random guessing, PPL equals the vocabulary size; with perfect prediction it is 1.\n- Example: with only 1/4 confidence at every step, PPL = 4. Monitoring validation loss during training is essentially monitoring log PPL.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nUses:\n- Comparing degradation of the same model before and after quantization, pruning or distillation (how much PPL rises on a fixed corpus after quantization);\n- Data filtering (samples with abnormally high PPL may be noise);\n- A weak feature for detecting machine-generated text.\n\nBut it has clear limitations (not comparable across tokenizers, doesn't measure output quality, etc.), so today LLM applications are evaluated mainly with task-level metrics and rubric-based evaluation, and PPL has receded to an engineering metric for training and compression.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Be able to write the formula and explain \"effective branching factor.\"\n- Be able to hand-write the one-liner that computes PPL from token logprobs.\n- Proactively mention its limitations.","L1-1.11.2":"PPL's two core limitations: it is not comparable across tokenizers, and low PPL does not mean high output quality. This is a must-know interview point.\n\n**How it works**\n- **Tokenizer-dependent**: PPL is a per-token average, and with different vocabularies a token means different things. The finer the tokenization, the easier each token is to guess and the lower the PPL. So to compare models with different tokenizers, convert to **bits-per-byte (bpb)** or bits-per-character, normalizing by the raw byte count. nanochat uses bits per byte as its validation metric.\n- **Low PPL ≠ high quality**: PPL measures how well the model fits the distribution of reference text; it does not measure factuality, helpfulness, instruction following or reasoning ability. A model that only generates fluent nonsense can have very low PPL, and PPL is also artificially low on repetitive or templated text.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\n- During pre-training, held-out validation NLL / PPL / bpb is stable, cheap and high-resolution, making it the main monitoring metric; but across tokenizers you must use bpb.\n- The validation set must be deduplicated against training data and broken down by domain (code, math, multilingual); the aggregate loss can mask degradation in a single domain.\n- User-facing quality evaluation should rely on downstream tasks and rubric-based evaluation.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- When asked \"Model A has lower PPL than B — is it better?\": first ask whether the tokenizer and test corpus are the same.\n- Be able to explain the bpb conversion: total cross-entropy (in bits) divided by the number of bytes in the text.","L1-1.11.3":"Beyond being incomparable across tokenizers and not measuring quality, PPL has three easily overlooked limitations.\n\n**How it works**\n- **Depends on the test corpus**: PPL varies widely across domains; if the test text appeared in the training set (data contamination), PPL is artificially low and meaningless. So the validation set must be deduplicated against training data.\n- **Poorly suited to chat / instruction models**: after instruction tuning and RL post-training, a model's distribution has moved away from \"raw text continuation.\" Computing PPL on bare text without a chat template is an out-of-distribution evaluation; a higher number doesn't mean the model got worse.\n- **Long texts need sliding-window computation**: when text exceeds the context window, compute it segment by segment with a sliding window, keeping part of the preceding text as conditioning for each segment. The choice of window length and stride affects the result (a smaller stride gives each token more context, lowering PPL but costing more compute), and must be reported.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nThese factors mean PPL is only useful for relative comparisons under tightly controlled conditions (same model, same tokenizer, same corpus, same computation method). The typical use case is comparing degradation before and after quantization, not cross-model leaderboards.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- When asked \"why does PPL go up after post-training,\" answer distribution shift and the missing chat template.\n- Explain that sliding-window parameters affect PPL and must be reported.\n- Stress deduplication and contamination prevention.","L1-1.11.4":"Hallucination is when a model generates content that is **fluent and confident but factually wrong or unsupported by the given context**. Being able to explain the causes layer by layer matters far more than reciting the definition.\n\n**How it works**\nThe causes can be analyzed at seven levels:\n1. **Training objective**: trained to \"continue plausibly,\" not to \"be right\"; long-tail facts lack statistical support;\n2. **Evaluation and incentives**: when scoring is accuracy-only, guessing scores higher than admitting ignorance;\n3. **Internal mechanisms**: \"known entity\" features wrongly suppress the default refusal circuit;\n4. **Knowledge boundaries**: training cutoff, private data never seen, errors in the corpus itself;\n5. **Generation process**: error accumulation, sampling randomness, exposure bias;\n6. **Context**: irrelevant or conflicting retrieved content, lost in the middle, context rot, going along with false premises;\n7. **Post-training**: RLHF favors confident, complete answers.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nHallucination is the top risk in deploying LLM applications. The value of a layered analysis is that each layer maps to a different mitigation: RAG for knowledge boundaries, allowing abstention for incentives, reasoning and tool verification for error accumulation, low temperature and structured output for sampling, context engineering for context, and output validation as a final safety net.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Don't say \"hallucination is a bug.\" A more accurate framing: it is an inevitable product of a generative model being required to output under uncertainty, with incentives biased toward answering. It can't be eliminated, only reduced to a level the business can accept.\n- Name the metrics you would monitor: groundedness, citation accuracy, abstention rate.","L1-1.11.5":"The statistical and incentive causes of hallucination show that a model will fabricate even when its data is entirely correct — and current evaluations still reward fabrication.\n\n**How it works**\n- **Statistical level**: next-token prediction optimizes likelihood. For facts that appear repeatedly in the corpus, fitting the distribution coincides with stating the fact correctly; but for **long-tail facts** (names, dates, numbers seen only once or twice), the model lacks statistical support yet must still output a distribution, so it generates something that \"looks like an answer.\" OpenAI's September 2025 paper \"Why Language Models Hallucinate\" formalizes this: even with entirely correct training data, generation errors on arbitrary facts (such as someone's birthday) are statistically unavoidable, and the error rate of the generation task is lower-bounded by the error rate of a corresponding binary classification problem.\n- **Incentive level**: the same paper's central argument is that mainstream benchmarks score only accuracy, so a wrong answer and no answer both score 0, and guessing under uncertainty always has a higher expected score than saying \"I don't know.\" Optimized against such leaderboards and training signals, models naturally learn to guess confidently.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nThe authors argue for changing how mainstream evaluations score — penalizing wrong answers and allowing abstention — rather than just adding a few dedicated hallucination benchmarks. The engineering counterpart is to assign a higher cost to wrong answers in business evaluations and encourage the model to abstain when uncertain.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Use \"long-tail facts\" to explain why more correct data can't eliminate hallucination.\n- Use expected score to show how accuracy metrics reward guessing.\n- Propose an evaluation design that allows abstention and penalizes errors.","L1-1.11.6":"The mechanistic and process causes of hallucination focus on how the model's internal circuits and autoregressive generation itself lead to fabrication.\n\n**How it works**\n- **Internal mechanisms**: Anthropic's 2025 interpretability research observed inside Claude that \"refuse / don't know\" is a circuit that is on by default; when the model recognizes \"this is an entity I'm familiar with,\" the corresponding features suppress that circuit. If a name seems familiar but the model doesn't actually know the relevant facts, the suppression misfires and the model fabricates an answer.\n- **Error accumulation (snowballing)**: autoregressive generation can't go back; once something early is wrong, later text keeps fabricating to stay self-consistent.\n- **Exposure bias**: during training the prefix is always real text; at inference the prefix is the model's own output, and errors amplify once it drifts from the training distribution.\n- **Context conflicts**: retrieved content is irrelevant, contradictory or outdated; when parametric knowledge conflicts with the context, the model may stick stubbornly to its prior.\n- **Sycophancy**: when an instruction carries a false premise (e.g. asking for a paper's three contributions when it has only two), the model tends to go along rather than correct it.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nThese causes are the most relevant for RAG and agents. Mitigations include: reasoning models or higher effort plus self-checking and tool verification to break error accumulation; context engineering to remove conflicts and trim content; and explicitly allowing the model in the prompt to point out faulty premises.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- When explaining causes of hallucination, add the extra layer of \"internal circuit misfiring\" to show familiarity with interpretability research.\n- Give a concrete example of sycophancy.\n- Explain why error accumulation makes long generations more prone to hallucination.","L1-1.11.7":"This group of causes explains how hallucination arises in three stages: knowledge coverage, sampling and post-training.\n\n**How it works**\n- **Knowledge boundaries**: training data has a cutoff date; the model has never seen private data or real-time information; the training corpus itself contains errors, outdated content and contradictions. Asked about something beyond these boundaries, the model still produces a plausible-sounding answer.\n- **Sampling randomness**: high temperature and loose top-p raise the chance of picking low-probability tokens, and low-probability tokens are often wrong factual fragments; one wrong pick can then trigger error accumulation.\n- **Post-training**: RLHF-style methods use human preferences as reward, and human raters prefer confident, complete, fluent answers, which reinforces the behavior of \"make something up rather than say I don't know.\"\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nCorresponding mitigations:\n- Knowledge boundaries → RAG / search grounding, with cited sources required;\n- Sampling → low temperature, structured output (but low temperature can't remove the model's own wrong beliefs);\n- Post-training → encourage calibration and abstention in reward design and evaluation, with a higher cost for wrong answers.\n\nThese three map to three tunable stages — data, decoding and training — which makes it easy to present a systematic solution in an interview.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Explain why RLHF can make hallucination worse.\n- Distinguish \"the model doesn't know\" (knowledge boundary; use RAG) from \"the model knows but says it wrong\" (a sampling or context problem).\n- Low temperature is one mitigation, not a cure.","L1-1.11.8":"Hallucination can't be eliminated; it can only be reduced in frequency and impact to a level the business accepts, using a set of measures each matched to a cause.\n\n**How it works**\n- **RAG / search grounding with required citations**: addresses knowledge boundaries and long-tail facts by giving answers a verifiable basis.\n- **Allow and encourage abstention; evaluate with a higher cost for wrong answers**: addresses incentives by making \"I don't know\" a legitimate option.\n- **Reasoning models / higher effort, self-checking, tool verification**: addresses error accumulation by having the model check intermediate results before answering, or run code and call tools to confirm.\n- **Structured output, low temperature**: addresses sampling randomness.\n- **Context engineering**: trim, de-conflict, and place key information at the beginning and end, addressing context problems such as lost in the middle and context rot.\n- **Output-side validation**: fact-checkers, guardrails, groundedness detection, as a safety net.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nEvery measure has a cost: RAG adds latency and the risk of retrieval failure; high effort adds cost; abstention lowers coverage; output validation requires extra model calls. Real systems usually combine them and tier by business risk — abstain readily in high-risk scenarios, favor coverage in low-risk ones.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Answer along the \"cause → mitigation\" mapping rather than listing tricks.\n- Name the metrics you'd monitor in production: groundedness, citation accuracy, abstention rate.\n- Stress that output validation is the safety net, and safety-critical controls belong outside the model.","L1-1.12.1":"A reasoning model generates a thinking process (reasoning / thinking tokens) before giving its final answer. The thinking content is still autoregressively generated tokens; it simply isn't presented as the final answer.\n\n**How it works**\n- On a \"scratchpad,\" the model decomposes the problem, tries approaches, checks intermediate results and abandons dead ends, then answers.\n- Why it works: a single Transformer forward pass has a fixed compute depth of L layers, so problems needing many serial steps (multi-digit arithmetic, multi-hop reasoning, planning) can't be finished in one pass. Writing intermediate results as tokens and reading them back unrolls \"depth\" over \"time\": every extra token generated buys another L layers of computation.\n- This is the fundamental reason chain-of-thought works. Reasoning models internalize this capability through training (mainly reinforcement learning) rather than having it elicited by prompts.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\n- Reasoning models deliver large gains on multi-step tasks such as math, code and planning, and are the foundation of agent capabilities.\n- Costs: thinking tokens are billed as output, add latency and consume `max_tokens`; the model may overthink simple tasks.\n- Timeline: September 2024, OpenAI o1 is the first large-scale commercial reasoning model, with hidden thinking; January 2025, DeepSeek-R1 is open-sourced with visible thinking; 2025, hybrid reasoning goes mainstream; 2026, the model decides how much to think.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Use \"unrolling depth over time\" to explain why CoT works.\n- Distinguish prompt-elicited CoT from RL-trained reasoning ability.","L1-1.12.2":"Test-time compute is a broader concept than reasoning models: spending more compute at inference to get better results, instead of relying only on bigger models or more training.\n\n**How it works**\nFive forms:\n- **Serial: think longer** — generate more thinking tokens, e.g. vendors' extended / adaptive thinking and reasoning effort.\n- **Parallel: think several times** — sample multiple reasoning paths and then vote, pick the best or synthesize, e.g. self-consistency and vendors' \"Pro\" / \"Deep Think\" modes.\n- **Search** — tree search and backtracking over intermediate steps, e.g. beam / MCTS-style reasoning.\n- **Verification and tools** — run code and call tools to check one's own answer, i.e. the agent loop.\n- **Latent-space compute** — instead of writing intermediate results as tokens, run extra rounds of computation on hidden states, e.g. looped transformer / latent reasoning.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\n- It opens a scaling axis beyond training scale: the same model gets stronger on hard problems by spending more inference compute.\n- The cost is latency and expense growing linearly or even multiplicatively, so route by task difficulty instead of maxing out everywhere.\n- Latent-space compute makes the thinking process less readable, hurting monitorability.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Be able to name the five forms — serial, parallel, search, tools, latent space — with one example each.\n- Distinguish the scaling logic of test-time compute from train-time compute.\n- Explain, with cost in mind, when parallel sampling is worth it.","L1-1.12.3":"DeepSeek-R1 is a reasoning model open-sourced in January 2025. It marked the turning point where reasoning models went from closed black boxes to something that could be studied, distilled and self-hosted.\n\n**How it works**\n- **Open weights, visible thinking**: thinking content is presented in full inside `<think>…</think>`, whereas the earlier OpenAI o1 (September 2024) hid its thinking from users.\n- **Published training recipe**: the paper disclosed a method for training reasoning ability with reinforcement learning, showing that reasoning can be acquired mainly through RL rather than only through supervised long chain-of-thought data. The specific training methods are covered in the post-training chapter.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\n- Research: for the first time the community could fully reproduce, analyze and distill a strong reasoning model, spawning many open reasoning models and small distilled models based on R1.\n- Product: \"is the thinking visible?\" became a point of divergence in product design — visible thinking helps debugging, auditing and user trust, while hidden thinking protects training assets and prevents distillation.\n- Engineering: when self-hosting a reasoning model, you need to parse `<think>` tags reliably; in streaming, handle the tag arriving split across multiple tokens so thinking doesn't leak into the user-visible answer.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- State R1's two differences from o1: open weights and visible thinking.\n- Discuss the trade-off between visible and hidden thinking.\n- R1's place on the reasoning-model timeline: o1 → R1 → hybrid reasoning → adaptive thinking.","L1-1.12.4":"Hybrid reasoning means a single model can either answer directly or think first; in 2026 this evolved into adaptive thinking, where the model itself decides whether and how much to think, and the developer only provides a coarse effort level.\n\n**How it works**\n- In 2025 hybrid reasoning went mainstream: Qwen3 unified thinking / non-thinking modes in one model and introduced a thinking budget; GPT-5 (August 2025) made reasoning a default capability.\n- 2026 control interfaces (as of 2026-09): Anthropic uses adaptive thinking + `output_config.effort` (low / medium / high / xhigh / max), the old `budget_tokens` is no longer accepted on new models, and thinking is always on for Fable 5.1 and Opus 5.5; OpenAI uses `reasoning.effort`, with supported levels varying by model; the Gemini 3 series uses thinking level; DeepSeek V4 has Non-Think / Think High / Think Max; Kimi K3 always thinks, defaulting to `max`.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nThe common design: **effort is a behavioral signal, not a hard token budget**. Anthropic's docs explain that effort affects every token in the response — text, tool calls and thinking — and at low effort the model makes fewer, shorter tool calls; the real hard cap is still `max_tokens`, shared by thinking and text. This frees developers from setting budgets by hand, but it also means thinking length can't be controlled precisely; choosing a level requires evaluation.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Distinguish effort (a behavioral signal) from `max_tokens` (a hard cap).\n- Be able to describe the differences between at least two vendors' control interfaces, and remind that model cards should be reread before integration.","L1-1.12.5":"Qwen3 is the archetype of hybrid reasoning: one model, two output formats, switched via the chat template, plus an inference-side thinking budget.\n\n**How it works**\n- **Thinking Mode Fusion**: post-training has four stages — Long-CoT cold start → reasoning RL → Thinking Mode Fusion → general RL. The third stage continues fine-tuning on a mix of data with and without thinking so the model learns both formats; smaller models use strong-to-weak distillation.\n- **Switching**: the hard switch is the template parameter `enable_thinking`; the soft switch is `/think` or `/no_think` in the user message. Non-thinking mode is trained with an empty thinking block kept in place so both modes share a consistent output format.\n- **Thinking budget**: at inference, once thinking reaches the set limit it is cut off and a \"stop thinking\" instruction is inserted; the model then produces an answer based on the partial thinking so far, with no extra training needed.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\n- One set of weights serves both modes, so deployment needs just one set of weights and one KV cache pool; the cost is that the two distributions share capacity and may interfere, so each needs separate evaluation.\n- The smaller the budget, the closer to non-thinking mode; answer quality depends on whether the partial thinking is sufficient. Plot a \"budget–quality–cost\" curve on an eval set before deciding.\n- The model card recommends different sampling parameters for the two modes: thinking mode temperature 0.6, top-p 0.95, top-k 20, and no greedy decoding; non-thinking mode 0.7, 0.8, 20.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- \"It's not two models\" is the key point.\n- Later evolution: Qwen3.8 switched to `reasoning_effort` levels and preserves historical thinking by default.","L1-1.12.6":"Task budgets are a beta feature from Anthropic (introduced with Opus 4.7 on 2026-04-16): an advisory token budget for an entire agentic loop that lets the model pace itself on long tasks.\n\n**How it works**\n- Unlike the per-request `max_tokens`, a task budget applies to a whole task made up of many rounds of tool calls.\n- Knowing the total budget, the model can allocate effort accordingly: explore less and converge early when the budget is tight, verify more thoroughly when it is generous.\n- Like effort, it is a behavioral signal, **not a hard cap**: the model may exceed it; real hard limits must still be enforced via `max_tokens`, call-count limits or external orchestration logic.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\n- The cost of long-horizon agent tasks is hard to predict; task budgets offer a softer control than forcibly interrupting from outside, letting the model wrap up within budget instead of being cut off midway.\n- Limitation: since it is only advisory, it can't serve as a billing or safety guarantee; production systems still need hard token, step and time limits at the orchestration layer.\n- It reflects the common direction of 2026 control interfaces: from humans setting precise budgets to giving the model coarse signals and letting it adapt.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Distinguish task budget (advisory, whole loop), effort (behavioral signal) and `max_tokens` (per-request hard cap).\n- Explain why agent systems still need external hard limits.","L1-1.12.7":"The thinking tokens of reasoning models bring two engineering pitfalls: billing you can't see, and squeezing the output budget so responses get truncated.\n\n**How it works**\n- **Billed as output and possibly invisible**: thinking content may be omitted or returned only as a summary by default, but billing covers every thinking token the model actually generated, so \"visible tokens\" ≠ \"billed tokens.\" Both OpenAI's and Anthropic's docs state this explicitly; Anthropic reports thinking-token counts in `usage.output_tokens_details.thinking_tokens` for cost attribution.\n- **Consumes the context window and `max_tokens`**: thinking and text share the cap. Set it too low and the model may spend the whole budget thinking, leaving the text truncated or even empty. OpenAI's docs recommend reserving at least 25,000 tokens for \"reasoning + output\" while experimenting; Anthropic recommends setting `max_tokens` very high at high effort.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\n- Cost estimates can't be based on visible output alone, or they will be badly underestimated.\n- Before launch, specifically monitor the rate of responses that end due to length truncation (stop reason); this is a failure mode unique to reasoning models.\n- In multi-turn agents, thinking tokens also occupy context capacity and must be counted against the available input budget.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- \"Visible tokens ≠ billed tokens\" is a frequently tested point.\n- Describe the concrete symptoms of setting `max_tokens` too low: empty or truncated text.\n- Name monitoring items: thinking-token share and truncation rate.","L1-1.12.8":"Reasoning models change the latency profile: there may be a long thinking period before the first visible token, which places new demands on product experience design.\n\n**How it works**\n- For traditional models, TTFT is mainly determined by prefill and is usually short; a reasoning model must first generate many thinking tokens before visible output, and these go through the token-by-token decode phase, taking time proportional to thinking length.\n- So \"time to first visible token\" can be far longer than traditional TTFT, and it fluctuates widely with task difficulty and effort level.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\n- User perception: if the interface gives no feedback for a long time, it is perceived as frozen. Streaming UIs need to show a thinking summary or a progress indicator (e.g. \"Analyzing…\", staged summaries).\n- System design: timeout thresholds must be reset for reasoning models, or normal requests get killed; latency-sensitive steps (real-time chat, simple classification by sub-agents) should use low effort or non-reasoning mode.\n- In evaluation, record time to first visible token and end-to-end latency separately, and trade off \"quality–cost–latency\" together with accuracy and token cost.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Distinguish TTFT from \"time to first visible token.\"\n- Propose UI and timeout-setting countermeasures.\n- Explain, using effort routing, how to control thinking time in latency-sensitive scenarios.","L1-1.12.9":"In multi-turn conversations and tool calls, a reasoning model's thinking blocks must be passed back unchanged; otherwise the model loses the context of \"why it did this.\"\n\n**How it works**\n- Reasoning models also think between tool calls (interleaved thinking). If the thinking blocks in the history are dropped or rewritten in the next request, the model no longer knows why it called that tool or what it planned to do next.\n- For stateless calls, vendors use signatures or encrypted content so you can pass the thinking back without seeing the plaintext: e.g. OpenAI's encrypted reasoning items and the signature on Anthropic's thinking blocks.\n- Open models are following suit: in tool-call scenarios DeepSeek-V4 keeps all reasoning content across the whole conversation (including across user messages), while in ordinary chat it drops old reasoning when a new user message arrives; Kimi K3 requires passing back the complete assistant message, including `reasoning_content` and `tool_calls`, unchanged; Qwen3.8's `preserve_thinking` is on by default. By contrast, early Qwen3 kept only the final answers in multi-turn history.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\n- Not passing it back causes erratic behavior midway through tool chains, repeated calls or broken reasoning; rewriting thinking blocks may cause signature verification to fail.\n- Passing thinking back increases context usage and input cost, but it is a prerequisite for reasoning models to work correctly.\n- Rules differ by vendor, so message trimming and compaction logic at the framework layer must be aware of thinking blocks.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Explain why it must be passed back, and why unchanged (signatures).\n- Be able to cite how different models handle it, and say that model cards must be read before integration.","L1-1.12.10":"Higher effort isn't always better. The right approach is to run an effort sweep on your own eval set and then route tasks to different levels by difficulty.\n\n**How it works**\n- On simple tasks, high effort only adds cost and latency, and can even cause \"overthinking\" that complicates easy problems. Anthropic's note on Opus 4.7's `max` level: on most workloads it significantly increases cost for little quality gain, and on some structured-output tasks it can cause overthinking.\n- **Sweep method**: prepare a difficulty-stratified question set (e.g. several each of extraction / classification, multi-step calculation or logic, and open-ended questions that require planning), pick a model that supports effort and run it at each level, recording accuracy, output tokens (thinking vs. text separately), end-to-end latency and time to first visible token, producing a \"quality–cost–latency\" table.\n- **Routing**: low levels for classification, extraction and sub-agents; high levels for planning and complex debugging.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\n- Effort is the most direct lever for cost control; using the highest level across the board lets cost spiral.\n- Routing itself requires judging task difficulty, either statically by task type or dynamically with a lightweight classifier; routing mistakes cost quality on hard problems.\n- Note that switching the top-level effort may invalidate the prompt cache.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- When asked \"how do you choose the reasoning level,\" answer \"sweep on our own eval set, then route,\" not \"higher is better.\"\n- List the metrics to record during a sweep.","L1-1.12.11":"Prompting reasoning models differs from prompting traditional models: \"think step by step\" prompts are largely redundant, and prescribing detailed thinking steps may actually constrain the model.\n\n**How it works**\n- Traditional models needed prompts like \"Let's think step by step\" to elicit chain-of-thought; reasoning models have internalized reasoning through RL training and generate their own thinking, so such prompts add almost nothing.\n- Prescribing thinking steps in detail (\"do A first, then B, finally C\") limits the model's exploration of better solution paths and may perform worse than letting it think on its own.\n- A more effective approach is to state the **goal, constraints and acceptance criteria** clearly: what problem to solve, what hard limits apply and what counts as done, and let the model decide how to reason.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\n- When migrating from older models to reasoning models, many prompts need rewriting: remove redundant thinking guidance and add clear success criteria.\n- Since newer closed APIs have withdrawn sampling parameters, prompts and effort have become the main means of controlling behavior, so prompt design actually matters more.\n- You can still require a specific output format or ask for justification in the answer; that is different from prescribing the thinking process.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Explain why CoT prompts have limited effect on reasoning models.\n- Give the three elements of prompting a reasoning model: goal, constraints, acceptance criteria.\n- Distinguish \"constraining the output\" from \"constraining the thinking process.\"","L1-1.12.12":"Monitorability refers to the fact that a reasoning model's thinking exists as natural-language tokens that humans and monitoring systems can \"read\" to spot bad intent or anomalous behavior. But this property is not inherently robust.\n\n**How it works**\n- Thinking tokens are a useful debugging and auditing signal: you can see why the model called a tool, or whether it is contemplating a prohibited action.\n- The risk: if more computation happens in hidden states rather than being written out as tokens (e.g. looped transformer, latent reasoning), readability declines.\n- GPT-6 Astra, released in September 2026, sparked discussion of this: according to The Information, it uses recurrent-depth / looped transformer-style techniques, which OpenAI has not officially confirmed; Raschka, paraphrasing its system card, says the monitorability of its reasoning traces declined relative to the previous-generation GPT-5.6 Sol, mainly due to shorter, less informative reasoning traces (both are second-hand accounts).\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nThe implication for engineers: **don't treat the thinking content as a complete record of the model's actual computation**. It can aid auditing, but safety-critical controls should live outside the model — access control, sandboxing, output validation. Latent-space computation can improve efficiency but at the expense of readability, a tension in the development of test-time compute.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Explain why thinking tokens are not the same as the actual reasoning process.\n- When asked about agent safety design, stress the control layers outside the model.\n- Be able to mention the impact of latent-space reasoning on monitorability.","L1-1.13.1":"ViT (Vision Transformer, 2020) turns an image into a token sequence a Transformer can process. It underlies the vision side of nearly every VLM and determines \"how many tokens an image costs.\"\n\n**How it works**\n1. Cut the image into fixed-size patches (e.g. 16×16 pixels); a 224×224 image yields 14×14 = 196 patches.\n2. Flatten each patch and pass it through a linear layer (equivalent to a convolution with stride equal to the patch size) to get a d-dimensional vector, i.e. an \"image token.\"\n3. Add 2D or learnable positional encodings, optionally a `[CLS]` token, and feed into a bidirectional-attention Transformer encoder.\n\nCost formula: `image tokens ≈ (H / patch) × (W / patch)`; doubling the resolution quadruples the token count.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nViT has almost no image-specific inductive bias (CNNs have locality and translation equivariance), so it underperforms CNNs on small data but overtakes them after large-scale pre-training (e.g. CLIP and the DINO series). For LLM engineers, the most practical concern is cost: high-resolution screenshots and document scans produce many visual tokens, directly consuming context and money.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Be able to compute token counts by hand: 336×336 with patch 14 → 24×24 = 576 tokens.\n- Follow-up \"why does ViT need lots of data?\": weak inductive bias, so it must learn local structure from data.\n- Follow-up \"how does cost change when resolution doubles?\": about 4x the tokens, and attention compute grows quadratically on top of that.","L1-1.13.2":"CLIP (2021) uses contrastive learning on about 400 million image-text pairs to map images and text into the same semantic space, enabling zero-shot classification and image-text retrieval. SigLIP is its improved version and, since 2024, one of the most commonly used vision encoder families in VLMs.\n\n**How it works**\nDual-tower architecture: an image encoder and a text encoder each output a vector. For a batch of N pairs, push the cosine similarity of the N matching pairs up and the remaining N² − N pairs down using a symmetric InfoNCE loss: a softmax cross-entropy in each direction (image→text and text→image), averaged, with `s_ij = cos(v_i, t_j)` and a temperature τ controlling distribution sharpness. For zero-shot classification, write class names as \"a photo of a {label}\" and pick the text most similar to the image. SigLIP replaces the softmax contrastive loss with a pairwise sigmoid binary classification loss, no longer relying on huge batches for normalization.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nCLIP's training objective is \"whole image–whole sentence\" alignment, so it is insensitive to fine-grained spatial relations, counting and small-text OCR, which explains many typical errors of early VLMs. Its embeddings also aren't suited to pixel-level tasks; those need the dense features of DINOv2 / DINOv3.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Write out the structure of symmetric InfoNCE and explain that temperature τ and the number of negatives matter a lot.\n- SigLIP vs. CLIP: global softmax normalization vs. pairwise sigmoid; the latter is insensitive to batch size.\n- CLIP is the foundation of multimodal RAG (text-to-image search) and visual agents.","L1-1.13.3":"The problem a VLM (vision-language model) solves is how to feed visual information into an LLM. There are five mainstream architectures; the core differences are what form the visual information takes, at which layer it enters the model, and how much sequence length and training cost it requires.\n\n**How it works**\n- (a) Projector-based (the LLaVA line, most common): vision encoder (ViT / SigLIP) → MLP projector → visual tokens, concatenated with text tokens and fed into a decoder-only LLM.\n- (b) Resampler / Q-Former (BLIP-2, Flamingo's Perceiver Resampler): a small number of learnable queries cross-attend to visual features and output a fixed number (e.g. 32 / 64) of visual tokens.\n- (c) Cross-attention (Flamingo, Llama 3.2 Vision): cross-attention is inserted into several LLM layers, with text as the query reading visual features; it takes up no sequence length.\n- (d) Early fusion / native multimodal: image patches and text tokens share one sequence and are trained jointly from pre-training onward.\n- (e) Encoder-free: image patches go straight into the LLM through a single linear layer, with no separately pre-trained vision encoder.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nProjectors are simple and cheap to train, but high resolution yields thousands of visual tokens; resamplers use few tokens but lose detail, hurting OCR; cross-attention suits multi-image / video but requires changing the LLM's structure; early fusion has the richest interaction and the highest ceiling but needs large-scale joint pre-training; encoder-free is architecturally unified and simple to deploy, but also depends on large-scale training.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Be able to draw the data flow of (a) and describe its two-stage training.\n- Compare the five approaches along three axes: token cost vs. detail fidelity vs. training cost.","L1-1.13.4":"In projector-based VLMs (the LLaVA line), the projector is the bridge between the vision encoder and the LLM: the vectors output by the vision encoder differ from the LLM's token embeddings in both dimension and distribution, and the projector maps the former into token embeddings the LLM \"understands.\"\n\n**How it works**\nThe projector is usually a 1–2 layer MLP that learns a mapping from the visual feature space to the LLM embedding space, making each visual feature \"look like\" a text token embedding. Typical two-stage training:\n1. Alignment stage: freeze both the vision encoder and the LLM, train only the projector on image-text pairs (e.g. image captioning).\n2. Instruction-tuning stage: unfreeze the LLM (or add LoRA) and fine-tune jointly on multimodal instruction data so the model learns to answer image-related questions following instructions.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nThis approach reuses off-the-shelf vision encoders and LLMs and is cheap to train, making it the most common recipe for open VLMs. The cost is a large number of visual tokens (thousands at high resolution), which occupies context and increases inference cost; compared with early fusion, image-text interaction happens later and the ceiling is lower. Training only the projector first matters because it has few parameters and converges quickly, and avoids damaging the LLM's existing language abilities with noisy gradients early in alignment.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Why freeze both ends in stage one: to avoid damaging pre-trained abilities and learn only the space mapping.\n- Projector vs. resampler: the former maps patch by patch without compression; the latter compresses to a fixed number via queries.\n- Whether stage two uses full fine-tuning or LoRA depends on data volume and compute.","L1-1.13.5":"Fixed-resolution vision encoders (e.g. 336×336) can't read small text in documents and screenshots. High-resolution dynamic tiling is a common way for VLMs to handle documents, charts and UI screenshots, trading more tokens for detail.\n\n**How it works**\n1. Based on the original size and aspect ratio, split the large image into several tiles, each resized to the encoder's input resolution and encoded separately.\n2. Add a thumbnail of the whole image, encoded separately, to provide global layout information.\n3. Feed the visual tokens of all tiles plus the thumbnail tokens into the LLM (usually with separator or position markers).\nAnother approach is to use an encoder that supports arbitrary resolution and cut patches directly at the original size.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nTiling lets the model read small text and details, which is crucial for OCR, tables and computer-use screenshots. The cost is a multiplied token count: by ViT's formula `tokens ≈ (H/patch) × (W/patch)`, each extra tile adds a full set of visual tokens, so a single high-resolution screenshot can run to thousands of tokens, directly affecting context usage, latency and cost. In practice you often need to cap the trade-off between \"seeing clearly\" and \"cost,\" e.g. by limiting the maximum number of tiles or downsampling first.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Why add a thumbnail: tiling loses global structure, and the thumbnail restores the overall layout.\n- Cost estimate: 4 tiles plus 1 thumbnail is roughly 5x the visual tokens of a single image.\n- Alternative: a resampler compresses tokens but sacrifices fine-grained abilities like OCR.","L1-1.13.6":"Two trends in VLM architecture in 2025–2026: open flagship models are moving from a \"bolted-on vision encoder\" to early fusion that mixes visual tokens in from pre-training; and among small and mid-sized models, encoder-free designs that drop the standalone vision encoder have appeared.\n\n**How it works**\n- Early fusion: image patches and text tokens are trained jointly in the same sequence from pre-training onward. Kimi K2.5 (2026-01) mixes in visual tokens during pre-training; Qwen3.5 (2026-02) merged the previously separate VL branch into the main line, emphasizing a unified vision-language base.\n- Encoder-free: Gemma 4 12B Unified (2026-06-03, 11.95B parameters, 256K context) removes the standalone vision and audio encoders. On the vision side, a lightweight module of \"one matrix multiply + position embedding + normalization\" projects patches into the embedding space; on the audio side, the raw audio signal is projected directly to the same dimension as text tokens.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nEarly fusion gives the richest cross-modal interaction and a high capability ceiling but needs large-scale joint pre-training; encoder-free is architecturally unified and simple to deploy but also depends on large amounts of training data. Not every flagship has vision: Qwen3.8-2.4T-A95B doesn't support multimodal input, GLM-5.2 is text-only, while Qwen3.8-27B natively supports images and video.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- \"Hybrid attention + MoE + native multimodality\" has become the new standard configuration for open flagship models.\n- When selecting a model, check the model card to confirm it really supports image input; don't infer it from the series name.","L1-1.13.7":"DeepSeek-OCR (2025-10) proposed \"contexts optical compression\": render long text as images, then compress them into a few visual tokens, using the visual channel to carry textual information. This offers a new idea for long-context compression and memory forgetting.\n\n**How it works**\n1. Render long text as an image.\n2. Use DeepEncoder to encode the image and compress it into a small number of visual tokens.\n3. A small MoE decoder recovers the text from the visual tokens (OCR).\nThe paper reports about 97% OCR accuracy at compression ratios below 10x and about 60% at 20x. In other words, under moderate compression one visual token can carry the information of about 10 text tokens nearly losslessly.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nThe core cost of long context lies in token count and KV cache. If old conversation history could be rendered as images and stored with fewer visual tokens, you get a tunable lossy compression: older content is compressed harder and becomes blurrier, much like human memory fading. Compare it with summarization / compaction: summaries rely on the model rewriting content, may lose key details and are irreversible; optical compression preserves the original layout, but accuracy drops sharply at high compression ratios. It is still a research direction, not a mature production solution.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Remember the two numbers: <10x ≈ 97%, 20x ≈ 60%.\n- Follow-up \"how does it differ from summary-based compaction?\": fidelity, controllable compression ratio, and whether it depends on generation.","L1-1.13.8":"The input side of multimodality is expanding to more modalities, and closed flagship models now commonly accept several input types natively. For agent engineering, the most important application of multimodality is computer use.\n\n**How it works**\nThe Gemini series natively accepts text, image, video, audio and PDF input, with unified token billing and context management. The open side is following: Gemma 4's E2B / E4B / 12B support audio input; Qwen3.8-27B natively supports images and video. Embeddings are going multimodal too: Gemini Embedding 2 maps text, images, video, audio and PDF into the same vector space.\nThe basic computer-use loop: the agent takes a screenshot → the model reads the image to understand the interface → outputs actions such as clicks and typing → takes another screenshot to verify. It depends on the model's fine-grained understanding of UI screenshots (small text, button positions).\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nThe more modalities, the more business inputs can be handled directly (contract PDFs, meeting recordings, product videos), removing separate OCR / ASR pipelines. But each modality differs in token cost, latency and accuracy, and screenshot-style inputs are especially expensive. GPT-5.4 has built computer use into its main model, showing that it is moving from specialized models into general-purpose ones.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- When asked \"what does multimodality do for agents,\" answer computer use first.\n- The trade-off between feeding PDF / audio directly vs. converting to text first: fewer pipelines vs. cost and controllability.","L1-1.13.9":"When wiring images and other multimodal inputs into LLM applications, there are three easily overlooked engineering issues: cost, security and evaluation.\n\n**How it works**\n- Cost: images are expensive inputs; a single high-resolution screenshot can cost thousands of tokens. By the ViT formula `tokens ≈ (H/patch) × (W/patch)`, doubling resolution roughly quadruples tokens, and dynamic tiling multiplies them further. Countermeasures: control resolution per task, crop key regions, limit the number of tiles, and keep only necessary screenshots in the agent's history.\n- Security: text in images is also read by the model and may be treated as instructions — prompt injection can hide in images (web page screenshots, uploaded document images). Countermeasures: treat image content as untrusted data, isolate it from tool permissions, and require confirmation for high-risk actions.\n- Evaluation: multimodal ability must be evaluated separately; rankings on text leaderboards don't transfer directly to vision tasks, and you should also test by task type (OCR, charts, UI grounding).\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nIn scenarios like computer use, screenshots accumulate at every step, so cost and context pressure grow quickly; and since screenshots come from external web pages, they are a natural entry point for injection attacks.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Estimate the visual token cost of a computer-use task.\n- Give an example of image-based injection and its defenses: separating data from instructions, least privilege, human confirmation.\n- Stress using your own multimodal eval set for model selection.","L1-1.14.1":"Embedding models and generative models (LLMs) are both Transformer-based but serve completely different purposes: a generative model outputs a probability distribution token by token, while an embedding model outputs one fixed-length vector in a single forward pass to measure semantic similarity. Embeddings are the retrieval foundation of RAG.\n\n**How it works**\n- Output: an LLM outputs a distribution over the next token and generates token by token; an embedding model outputs a fixed-length vector (e.g. 768 / 1024 / 3072 dimensions).\n- Training objective: LLMs use next-token prediction (plus post-training); embeddings use contrastive learning, pulling vectors of similar texts together and pushing dissimilar ones apart.\n- Attention: LLMs use causal, unidirectional attention; embedding models are traditionally bidirectional encoders, though LLM-based ones exist too.\n- Whole-text representation: obtained via pooling, commonly `[CLS]`, mean pooling or the last token.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nEmbeddings are low in cost and latency and can be computed offline in batches and indexed; typical uses are retrieval, clustering, deduplication, classification and recommendation. Generative models' cost grows with output length; they are used for conversation, reasoning and generation. In RAG they divide the labor: embeddings handle recall, and the LLM answers based on the retrieved results.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- This is a prerequisite question in RAG interviews; be able to contrast output, objective, attention and pooling in table form.\n- Common follow-ups: why can't you use LLM hidden states directly as sentence vectors; the difference between bi-encoders and cross-encoders.","L1-1.14.2":"Using the hidden state of some LLM layer or of the last token directly as a sentence vector for retrieval usually works poorly. There are three reasons, and understanding them explains why embedding models need dedicated training.\n\n**How it works**\n1. **Objective mismatch**: next-token prediction optimizes \"can the last position predict the next word,\" and does not require two semantically similar sentences to be close in vector space.\n2. **Causal attention**: representations at earlier positions can't see later text; only the last token has seen the whole sentence, but it is trained as a representation for \"predicting the next word,\" not for \"summarizing the sentence's meaning.\"\n3. **Anisotropy**: representations without contrastive training tend to be crowded into a narrow cone, so any two sentences have high cosine similarity and discrimination is poor.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nThe fix isn't to abandon LLMs but to fine-tune them into embedding models with contrastive learning: take the last (EOS) token or use mean pooling, and some approaches remove the causal mask to make attention bidirectional. This keeps the LLM's world knowledge and multilingual ability while gaining a vector space suited to retrieval. It also shows that \"representation\" and \"generation\" are two different optimization objectives.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Have the three reasons on the tip of your tongue: objective, causality, anisotropy.\n- Follow-up \"so how are LLM-based embeddings built?\": contrastive fine-tuning + pooling strategy + optional bidirectional attention.\n- Follow-up \"how is anisotropy mitigated?\": contrastive learning itself spreads representations out, distributing them uniformly in the space.","L1-1.14.3":"Contrastive learning is the core method for training embedding models, structurally the same as CLIP except both sides are text: pull related (query, document) vectors together and push unrelated ones apart. The quality of hard negatives is the key to performance.\n\n**How it works**\n- Positive pairs: from labeled data, Q&A pairs, and weak supervision signals such as title–body.\n- Negatives: documents from other samples in the same batch (in-batch negatives), at almost zero cost; plus specially mined **hard negatives** — documents that look similar to the query on the surface but are actually irrelevant (e.g. retrieve the top-k with BM25 or an older model, then remove the truly relevant ones).\n- Loss: InfoNCE, `−log(exp(s⁺/τ) / Σ exp(s/τ))`, where s is similarity and τ is temperature. Temperature τ and the number of negatives strongly affect results.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nRandom negatives are too easy to distinguish, so the model quickly stops learning; hard negatives force it to learn fine-grained semantic distinctions and directly determine retrieval precision. But hard negatives may include \"false negatives\" (actually relevant but unlabeled), which send wrong signals and need filtering or verification with a cross-encoder. Larger batches give more in-batch negatives but cost more memory.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Explain where positives come from and the difference between in-batch and hard negatives.\n- Follow-up \"how do you mine hard negatives, and what are the pitfalls?\": filter after top-k retrieval, watch for false negatives.\n- Follow-up on the role of temperature τ: it controls distribution sharpness and how much attention goes to hard examples.","L1-1.14.4":"LLM-based embedding fine-tunes a decoder-only LLM into an embedding model with contrastive learning. It has become the mainstream approach for modern embedding models, e.g. the Qwen3-Embedding series (0.6B / 4B / 8B, June 2025).\n\n**How it works**\n1. Use a pre-trained LLM as the backbone and fine-tune it with contrastive learning (positive pairs + in-batch negatives + hard negatives).\n2. Pooling: take the hidden state of the last (EOS) token or mean-pool all tokens; since under causal attention only the last position has seen the whole sentence, last-token pooling is common. Some approaches remove the causal mask to get bidirectional attention.\n3. Instruction-aware: prepend a task instruction to the query (e.g. \"Retrieve passages relevant to this question\"), so the same model can adapt to retrieval, classification, clustering and other tasks; queries and documents usually use different prefixes.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nIt benefits from the LLM's world knowledge and multilingual ability and is usually stronger on multilingual, long-text and complex semantics. The cost is that it is much heavier than BERT-style models, with higher latency and cost; for latency- and cost-sensitive scenarios, a bidirectional encoder with a few hundred million parameters (e.g. ModernBERT-based models) remains a pragmatic choice. With instruction-aware models, you must add the correct instruction prefix to queries per the model card, or performance drops noticeably.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- The trade-off between last-token and mean pooling, and why.\n- Why queries and documents use different prefixes: their distributions are asymmetric.\n- When to choose a small encoder over an LLM embedding: latency, cost, throughput.","L1-1.14.5":"Matryoshka Representation Learning (MRL, \"Russian nesting doll\" representations) makes the first k dimensions of a high-dimensional vector a usable representation in their own right, so vectors can be truncated on demand to flexibly trade off storage, retrieval speed and accuracy.\n\n**How it works**\nDuring training, the contrastive loss is computed not only on the full vector but simultaneously on several prefix lengths (e.g. 64, 128, 256, 768, full length) and summed with weights, forcing the most important semantic information into the leading dimensions, nested like matryoshka dolls. At use time, simply take the first k dimensions (usually followed by another L2 normalization), with no retraining. For example, truncating 3072 dimensions to 768 or 256 loses very little accuracy. Gemini Embedding 2 defaults to 3072 dimensions and supports MRL truncation.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nVector dimension × number of documents determines storage and retrieval cost, and the difference is huge at hundreds of millions of documents. MRL lets the same model serve different cost tiers and enables two-stage retrieval: coarse recall with short vectors, then reranking with full-length vectors. The cost is that shorter truncation loses more accuracy, so the truncation dimension must be determined by evaluation on your own data; models not trained with MRL can't be truncated arbitrarily.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Explain why truncation works: a joint loss over multiple prefixes during training.\n- Applications: lower storage; short-vector coarse recall + long-vector reranking.\n- Follow-up: should you renormalize after truncation? (Yes, especially when using cosine similarity.)","L1-1.14.6":"Bi-encoders and cross-encoders are two complementary scoring structures in retrieval systems: the former is fast and indexable, used for large-scale recall; the latter is accurate but expensive, used to rerank a small set of candidates.\n\n**How it works**\n- **Bi-encoder** (i.e. an embedding model): the query and document are encoded independently into vectors, and similarity is just the dot product or cosine of the two vectors. Document vectors can be precomputed and indexed (e.g. an ANN index), making retrieval extremely fast; but there is no token-level interaction between query and document.\n- **Cross-encoder** (reranker): the query and document are concatenated and fed into the model together, attention interacts fully between them, and the model outputs a relevance score directly — more accurate, but every pair needs its own forward pass and nothing can be precomputed.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nWith millions of documents, running a cross-encoder forward pass on every document is impractical, so typical RAG uses two stages: a bi-encoder (often in hybrid retrieval with BM25) recalls the top-k (e.g. 50–100), then a cross-encoder reranks them into the top-n handed to the LLM. This balances speed and accuracy. Reranking adds latency, and the candidate count k must balance recall against latency.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- One-line difference: independent encoding + dot product vs. concatenation + interaction.\n- Why cross-encoders can't search the whole corpus: every pair needs a forward pass, and nothing can be indexed.\n- Follow-up \"how much latency does reranking add, and how do you choose k?\": decide using recall and end-to-end latency curves.","L1-1.14.7":"Switching embedding models isn't as simple as changing one line of config: vector spaces of different models are incompatible, so a new model means recomputing all vectors and rebuilding indexes. Model versions must therefore be managed together with index versions.\n\n**How it works**\nEach embedding model learns its own vector space, with different dimensions, distributions and semantic coordinates; even with the same dimension, computing similarity between a new model's query vector and an old model's document vectors is meaningless. So the rollout process is usually:\n1. Evaluate the new model on your own data (leaderboards like MTEB are only a reference, subject to overfitting and domain bias).\n2. Recompute all document vectors offline with the new model and build a new index.\n3. Run the old and new indexes in parallel, switch via canary or A/B, and retire the old index once metrics are confirmed.\n4. Record the model name and version for each index in metadata, and have the query side strictly encode queries with the same version.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nWith large document volumes, the recomputation cost (compute, time, double storage) is considerable, so be careful at selection time and consider how dimension affects storage and retrieval cost; MRL models can control cost via truncation. Multilingual scenarios (mixed Chinese-English, Southeast Asian languages) need separate validation before switching.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Why they can't be mixed: incompatible vector spaces.\n- Migration plan: dual writes / dual indexes, canary switchover, version binding.\n- Selection: evaluation on your own data takes priority over leaderboards; watch multilingual performance and cost.","L1-1.15.1":"SSMs (structured state space models) and Mamba try to summarize all history in a fixed-size state, escaping attention's problems of a KV cache that grows with length and total compute that grows quadratically with length.\n\n**How it works**\nMamba (December 2023) updates the hidden state via a linear recurrence: `h_t = A_t h_{t−1} + B_t x_t`. The key innovation is \"selectivity\": A and B depend on the current input, so the model can decide what to remember and what to forget. Traditional RNNs are hard to train in parallel and have poor long-range memory; Mamba uses a parallel scan algorithm to parallelize training, and at inference each step is O(1), with state size independent of sequence length. Follow-ups include Mamba-2 and Mamba-3, accepted at ICLR 2026 (improved discretization, complex-valued state updates and a multi-input multi-output formulation).\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nA fixed-size state brings constant inference cost and memory, ideal for ultra-long context and streaming; but it is lossy compression and can't look back at the original text precisely, so copying and retrieval tasks suffer. Hence pure SSMs haven't replaced Transformers but have entered flagship models as part of hybrid architectures: for example, Nemotron 3 is mainly Mamba-2 with only a few GQA layers.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Write the recurrence and explain that \"selectivity\" means the parameters depend on the input.\n- Cover both points: parallel training (scan) and O(1) inference.\n- \"Will Mamba replace Transformers?\": pure SSMs haven't, but recurrent modules have entered flagship models as part of hybrid architectures.","L1-1.15.2":"The linear attention / delta rule family (Gated DeltaNet, Kimi Delta Attention or KDA, etc.) reaches the same destination as SSMs by a different road: compress history into a fixed-size \"fast weight\" matrix to get O(1) per-token inference cost. It is the most common recurrent layer in 2025–2026 hybrid architectures.\n\n**How it works**\nRemove the softmax from attention and `Σ v_i k_iᵀ` can be accumulated into a d×d state matrix S, queried as `o_t = S_t q_t`. These are the \"fast weights\": the context is written into a matrix that updates over time. Naive linear attention only writes additively and is prone to interference; the delta rule instead \"reads out the old value first, then corrects by the error,\" equivalent to one step of online regression, with gating to control forgetting — i.e. a gated delta rule.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nThese layers need no KV cache that grows with sequence length, so long-context inference is cheap. Representative uses: Qwen3.5 / 3.8 mix Gated DeltaNet and Gated Attention at 3:1; Kimi Linear and Kimi K3 mix KDA with MLA, and the Kimi Linear paper reports up to 75% less KV cache than a full-MLA baseline and up to about 6x decoding throughput at 1M context. The cost is again lossy compression, so a few global attention layers must be kept for precise retrieval.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Explain \"fast weights\": the state matrix is a compressed memory of the context.\n- The advantage of the delta rule over purely additive writes: it can correct and forget, reducing interference.\n- Be able to cite Qwen3.5's 3:1 ratio and Kimi Linear's numbers.","L1-1.15.3":"The fundamental trade-off of SSMs and linear attention: a fixed-size state is lossy compression. This is why they can't replace attention on their own, and why 2026 flagship models generally adopt hybrid architectures.\n\n**How it works**\nAttention keeps the K and V of every past token, so it can precisely \"look back at the original text of token 3,712\"; recurrent modules have only a fixed-size state, so information from long sequences is inevitably compressed and overwritten. Tasks needing precise copying and retrieval suffer as a result, such as quoting the original text, long identifiers in code, and in-context learning.\nThe division of labor in hybrid architectures: most layers use linear / recurrent modules for \"cheap context aggregation,\" while a few layers keep full attention for \"precise retrieval.\" For example, Qwen3.5 is 15 × (3 × DeltaNet + 1 × Attention); Kimi K3 has 69 KDA layers + 24 Gated MLA layers; Nemotron 3 is mainly Mamba-2 with a few GQA layers; Ling 2.5 uses Lightning Attention + MLA.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nHybrid architectures greatly reduce KV cache and compute at million-token context while preserving retrieval ability with a few global layers; the cost is more complex architecture and inference-engine support, and new architectures are prone to problems early in engine adaptation.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- The standard answer to \"will Mamba replace Transformers?\": pure SSMs haven't, but recurrent modules have entered flagship models as part of hybrid architectures.\n- State that \"the backbone is still a Transformer, but the attention layers are now hybrid,\" and the driver: KV cache and compute cost at long context.","L1-1.15.4":"Diffusion language models (Diffusion LMs) try to break the serial bottleneck of autoregressive \"one token at a time\" generation: predict all positions in parallel, then refine iteratively. You can think of it as \"a BERT with a variable masking ratio that can be applied iteratively.\"\n\n**How it works**\nFor text, discrete / masked diffusion is mainstream:\n- Training: randomly replace a portion of tokens with `[MASK]`, with the ratio varying from 0 to 100%, and have the model predict the masked tokens.\n- Generation: start from an all-`[MASK]` sequence; at each step predict all positions in parallel, keep the high-confidence results and re-mask the rest, iterating for several steps until everything is settled.\nA representative work is LLaDA (Large Language Diffusion Models). Real systems often use block diffusion, denoising block by block in parallel; e.g. DiffusionGemma denoises a 256-token canvas in parallel each time.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nParallel generation can make it an order of magnitude faster, and every step can modify any position, making it naturally suited to infilling and editing. Costs: output length usually has to be fixed in advance or handled in blocks; there is no mature KV cache mechanism, so each step recomputes the whole sequence; quality is currently on par with lightweight models, and the ecosystem for streaming output, tool calling and so on is still being built.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Explain the principle in one line: \"an iteratively applied BERT.\"\n- Lay out training (variable-ratio masking) and generation (all masked → keep by confidence → iterate).\n- Follow-up \"why hasn't it replaced autoregression?\": quality, caching, variable length, ecosystem.","L1-1.15.5":"Diffusion LMs and autoregressive LMs are two different generation paradigms. Comparing them comes down to the trade-off between speed and editability on one side and quality and ecosystem on the other.\n\n**How it works**\n- Generation: autoregressive is serial, token by token; diffusion LMs work on many tokens in parallel with iterative denoising.\n- Speed: autoregressive is limited by serialization; diffusion LMs can be an order of magnitude faster.\n- Revising generated content: autoregressive can't go back; diffusion supports it natively, since every step can change any position, suiting infilling and editing.\n- Output length: naturally variable for autoregressive; diffusion usually needs it fixed in advance or handled in blocks.\n- Caching: autoregressive KV cache and prefix caching are mature; diffusion recomputes the whole sequence at every step, with a different caching mechanism.\n- Quality and ecosystem: autoregressive is at the frontier, with mature streaming, tool calling and structured output; diffusion currently matches lightweight models, and its ecosystem is still being built.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nDiffusion LMs suit extremely latency-sensitive scenarios: code completion, the text stage of voice interaction, real-time rewriting. They are not yet suitable as the main model for complex agents, since multi-step tool calling needs high-quality reasoning and a mature tool-calling ecosystem. When selecting models, treat them as an alternative \"low-latency lightweight model\" and evaluate them head to head with small autoregressive models.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Be able to compare along 4–5 dimensions.\n- Give one scenario where it fits and one where it doesn't.\n- Follow-up \"why is caching hard for diffusion?\": the whole sequence changes at every step, so you can't just append as with a KV cache.","L1-1.15.6":"Diffusion LLMs have moved from research to usable products: Inception's Mercury series is currently the main commercial diffusion LLM, and Google DeepMind's DiffusionGemma is the first open-weight dLLM supported by vLLM.\n\n**How it works**\n- Mercury: the first generation was released with a paper in 2025; Mercury 2 launched on 2026-02-24, officially about 1,009 tokens/s on Blackwell GPUs with 128K context, supporting adjustable reasoning depth, tool calling and schema-conformant JSON output, priced at $0.25 / $0.75 per million tokens; Mercury 2.5 launched on 2026-09-08, officially about 1,107 tokens/s with 260K context, with intelligence comparable to cost-optimized models such as GPT-5.6 Luna (Low), Gemini 3.5 Flash-Lite and Claude Haiku 4.5, priced at $0.20 / $0.75. All of these are vendor claims.\n- DiffusionGemma (2026-06-10): a 26B block-diffusion model built on the Gemma 4 backbone (model name A4B, about 4B active) that denoises a 256-token canvas in parallel each time; vLLM says it reaches 1,288 tokens/s at batch size 1 on H200 (FP8). Licensed under Apache 2.0 (second-hand report; defer to the model page).\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nThey show that the speed advantage of diffusion LLMs can be realized in practice, and they are starting to fill in capabilities like tool calling and JSON output; but in quality they are still positioned in the lightweight / cost-optimized tier, not as frontier flagships.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- When citing speed numbers, add \"vendor-reported.\"\n- Positioning: comparable to lightweight models, suited to low-latency scenarios, not as the main model for complex agents.","L1-1.15.7":"A looped transformer shares weights across a set of layers and runs them multiple times, increasing effective depth without adding parameters; it moves part of the \"reasoning\" into hidden states, which is why it is also called latent reasoning. The idea traces back to the 2018 Universal Transformer.\n\n**How it works**\nLoop N blocks k times to get N×k block applications; the number of loops can also be chosen adaptively based on token difficulty. For example, Nanbeige4.2-3B loops 22 blocks twice for 44 block applications, and ByteDance's Ouro series loops 48 blocks four times. Relation to reasoning models: explicit reasoning writes intermediate computation out as tokens (unrolling depth over time), while looped depth lets more serial computation happen in hidden states without writing it out.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nIt saves parameters, not compute: forward and backward passes still go through every loop, so compute is comparable to an ordinary model of the same effective depth; each loop iteration needs its own KV cache, so cache isn't saved either. Academic evidence (preprints): TRM reaches about 45% on ARC-AGI-1 with 7M parameters; LOTUS claims to match explicit CoT for the first time at 3B scale while cutting thinking-phase latency by 2.5–6.9x; SMELT saves 6.8%–18.0% of training FLOPs on the compute-optimal frontier. The flip side is monitorability: reasoning is no longer written as tokens, so it is hard to inspect. Whether GPT-6 Astra uses this technique is only reported by the media; OpenAI has not officially confirmed it.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Key conclusion: saves parameters, not compute or KV cache.\n- The trade-off vs. explicit CoT: latency / parameter efficiency vs. interpretability and monitorability.\n- When mentioning GPT-6 Astra, always say \"reportedly.\"","L1-1.16.1":"As of 2026-09, closed flagship models come from five companies — OpenAI, Anthropic, Google, xAI and Meta — and their main models mostly have around 1M context. Knowing the landscape helps you give well-grounded answers to model-selection questions, though model release cycles have shrunk to 1–3 months.\n\n**How it works**\n- OpenAI: GPT-6 Astra (09-03), GPT-6 Sol / Luna (09-22), GPT-6.1 Sol (09-29), all 1.05M / 128K; Astra is the strongest, Sol the best value, Luna the high-throughput option; `reasoning.effort` goes up to `max`.\n- Anthropic: Claude Fable 5.1 (09-01), Opus 5.5 (09-22), Sonnet 5.5 (09-28), 1M / 128K; Haiku 4.5 is 200K / 64K; adaptive thinking + effort.\n- Google: Gemini 3.8 Flash (09-02); the Pro line is Gemini 3.1 Pro (preview), both 1,048,576 / 65,536; native multimodality and thinking level.\n- xAI: Grok 4.7 (500K; date from second-hand reports).\n- Meta: the closed Muse Spark, marking its move away from Llama's open-weight approach.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nThe context window is no longer a differentiator (Grok 4.7's 500K is the exception); differences have shifted to effective utilization of long context, cache pricing, reasoning levels and tool ecosystems. Many entries come from second-hand reports and should be checked against official docs.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- When citing models, add \"as of the last time I checked.\"\n- Distinguish \"officially confirmed\" from \"reportedly,\" e.g. Gemini 3.5 Pro has been announced but had not launched as of 09-30.\n- Base selection on your own evaluations, pricing and caching, tool ecosystem and compliance, not leaderboards.","L1-1.16.2":"Open-weight and Chinese flagship models are the source of models you can self-host, fine-tune and study architecturally. As of 2026-09, the main ones include DeepSeek, Kimi, Qwen, GLM, MiniMax, Gemma, Muse Glimmer, Inkling, Mistral, gpt-oss, Nemotron and Llama 4.\n\n**How it works**\nA few representatives (sizes are total / active parameters):\n- DeepSeek-V4-Pro 1.6T / 49B, 1M context, CSA + HCA hybrid attention, mHC, Muon optimizer, MIT; V4.1-Flash 552B, Causal Encoder-Decoder and FP4 KV cache.\n- Kimi K3 2.8T / 104B, 69 KDA layers + 24 Gated MLA layers, custom license.\n- Qwen3.5 397B / 17B, Qwen3.8 2.4T / 95B and 27B dense, Gated DeltaNet + Gated Attention at 3:1.\n- GLM-5 744B / 40B, MLA + DSA, MIT; GLM-5.2 is 1M text-only.\n- Gemma 4 (Apache 2.0), Muse Glimmer 30B (32 query heads / 2 KV heads), gpt-oss (Apache 2.0, MXFP4, harmony format), Nemotron 3 (Mamba-2 hybrid MoE).\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nOpen flagship models are now trillion-parameter MoEs, so single-machine self-hosting is impractical; architectural innovation happens mainly on the open side, concentrated in attention and KV cache. Licenses vary widely: MIT / Apache 2.0 are safe for commercial use, while custom licenses must be read clause by clause.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Use open models as examples when discussing architectural details (closed models largely don't disclose them).\n- Be able to describe the architectural highlights and licenses of 2–3 models.\n- Some specs are second-hand; say they are \"to be verified.\"","L1-1.16.3":"Two conclusions can be read from the 2026 model landscape: the interfaces of the three closed vendors have converged; and open flagship models are trillion-parameter MoEs, so what you can actually self-host and fine-tune are ~30B-class models.\n\n**How it works**\n- **Converging interfaces**: OpenAI, Anthropic and Google all offer 1M context, 64K–128K-class output (Gemini 65,536; OpenAI and Anthropic 128K), adaptive thinking + effort levels, and three-tier strong / medium / fast product lines. So selection depends more on eval results for the specific task, pricing and caching strategy, tool ecosystem and compliance requirements than on \"who has the bigger window.\"\n- **Open trillion-parameter MoEs**: DeepSeek-V4-Pro (1.6T), Kimi K3 (2.8T), Qwen3.8 (2.4T) and others can't realistically be self-hosted on a single machine; they need multi-node clusters or third-party inference services. What can actually be deployed and fine-tuned on your own GPUs are ~30B dense models and small MoEs, such as Qwen3.8-27B, Gemma 4, Muse Glimmer 30B and gpt-oss-20b.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nThis ~30B tier is the main battleground for \"LLM fine-tuning\" roles: memory is manageable, LoRA / full fine-tuning is feasible, and models can be deployed in your own environment to satisfy data residency. The value of trillion-scale open models lies more in research, access via hosted services, and serving as teachers for distillation.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- When asked \"what would you self-host?\": answer the ~30B class and explain the memory and operations constraints.\n- When asked \"how do you choose a closed model?\": interfaces have converged, so look at your own evals, pricing and caching, tooling and compliance.","L1-1.16.4":"In 2026, architectural innovation on the open side concentrated on attention and KV cache; but you should also know the counterexamples: some models' progress comes mainly from post-training, not architecture.\n\n**How it works**\n- **Architectural innovation**: MLA (compressed KV), DSA (DeepSeek Sparse Attention), CSA / HCA / CSA2 (DeepSeek-V4 / V4.1's compressed attention), KDA and Gated DeltaNet (linear attention), MiniMax's MSA (sparse attention). The driver is KV cache and compute cost at million-token context.\n- **Post-training counterexample**: Raschka's breakdown of Xiaomi MiMo-V2.6 Pro (2026-09-22) points out that its attention is just GQA plus a 128-token sliding window, and its gains came from post-training: broader agent task coverage, upgrading the reward from correctness checks to an agentic grader that inspects execution trajectories, and large-batch RL (1,568 prompts per step, 16 rollouts each). Similarly, GLM-5.3 and the official DeepSeek-V4-Flash release reportedly kept the same base and upgraded through post-training.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nArchitectural innovation mainly changes inference cost and long-context ability; post-training mainly changes agent and reasoning ability. Closed models largely don't disclose their architectures, so use open models as examples when discussing architectural details. For engineering roles, understanding \"where capability gains come from\" helps decide whether to switch models or invest in data and RL.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Be able to list 3–4 attention / KV innovations and their purposes.\n- Cite the MiMo-V2.6 Pro counterexample and explain the agentic grader and large-batch RL.","L1-1.16.5":"Licensing and data residency are the engineering constraints most easily overlooked in model selection and most likely to cause problems, and they are especially relevant for finance and government roles in Singapore.\n\n**How it works**\n- **Licenses**: MIT / Apache 2.0 are safe for commercial use (e.g. DeepSeek-V4, GLM-5, Gemma 4, gpt-oss); \"Modified MIT,\" \"Community License\" and vendors' custom licenses (e.g. the Kimi K3 License, the Qwen3.8-2.4T-A95B license) must be read clause by clause. Common terms include attribution requirements, revenue or user-count thresholds, and usage restrictions. Second-hand reports say the Kimi K3 License requires a separate agreement for MaaS operators with annual revenue over US$20 million and attribution above 100 million monthly active users (defer to the license text). Different sizes from the same company may also have different licenses; e.g. Qwen3.8-27B is Apache 2.0.\n- **Data residency**: the same model may differ in available regions and data retention policies between the vendor's direct API and various cloud platforms. Finance or government clients may require data to stay onshore, in which case self-hosting or a cloud provider's regional endpoint becomes a hard constraint.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nLicense violations are a legal risk, and non-compliant residency can veto a solution outright; these often decide model selection before a few percentage points of quality difference do.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Proactively raising licensing and residency in a model-selection question is a strong engineering signal.\n- Say you \"check licenses model by model\" rather than generalizing by vendor.\n- Options under compliance constraints: self-hosting, regional endpoints, managed inference services.","L1-1.16.6":"\"Closed API or self-hosted open model\" is a frequent scenario question. The right way to answer is not to pick a model first but to list constraints and decide with your own eval set; the usual conclusion is tiered routing rather than either-or.\n\n**How it works**\n1. Pin down constraints first: task difficulty and quality requirements, acceptable latency, cost ceiling per session, whether data can leave the country / company, traffic volume, and whether the team can operate inference infrastructure.\n2. Characteristics of each side: closed APIs have a high capability ceiling, a mature tool ecosystem and zero ops, but cost scales linearly with volume and you are subject to vendor changes; self-hosted open models are controllable, fine-tunable and keep data in-house, but need GPUs and ops capability, and trillion-scale flagships are hard to self-host.\n3. Tiered design: for a multilingual Southeast Asian customer-service agent, use a mid-tier closed flagship model with tuned effort for multi-step planning and complex tool calls; use lightweight models or self-hosted open models below the 30B class for high-volume, simple parts like intent classification, routing and FAQ rewriting, fine-tuned if needed.\n4. Add a model gateway layer to lower the cost of switching models.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nMultilinguality is a key risk: models vary widely on Thai, Vietnamese and Indonesian, and tokenizer compression rates also affect cost, so eval sets should be stratified by language. For compliance, confirm cloud regions and data retention policies.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- When fine-tuning a small model pays off: narrow and stable task, high traffic, latency-sensitive, high-quality data available.\n- Handling closed-model version changes: pin versions, run regression evals, switch via canary.\n- Cost estimation: compute input, cached, output and thinking tokens separately from the real token distribution; for agents, accumulate per step and include retries.","L1-1.16.7":"When a fine-tuned or freshly deployed open model performs clearly worse than expected, most \"model quality\" problems are actually serving configuration bugs. The debugging principle is to start with the cheapest and most common causes and only suspect data and training last.\n\n**How it works**\n1. **Chat template**: print the full string actually sent to the model and compare it character by character with the official template's rendering, looking for missing role markers, an extra BOS, or a missing assistant start marker. Mismatched training and inference templates are the most common cause.\n2. **Stop conditions**: if output won't stop or has odd trailing content, check whether EOS appears at the end of training samples and whether the inference framework's stop list is correct.\n3. **Generation config**: the framework's default sampling parameters may differ from the model card's recommendations; reproduce with greedy or the recommended config first. For models with a thinking mode, confirm the thinking switch and how historical thinking is handled.\n4. **Deployment**: check whether quantization has degraded quality, whether precision is appropriate, and whether the engine correctly supports structures such as sliding windows, special positional encodings and hybrid attention layers; compare logits from the reference implementation and the production engine on the same input.\n5. **Data and training**: data format, whether the loss is computed only on the response, learning rate, overfitting.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nOnly by holding a small eval set fixed throughout and changing one variable at a time can you pin down the root cause.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Distinguish engine problems from weight problems: compare per-token logits or greedy output against the reference implementation.\n- Rambling without stopping after fine-tuning: training data lacks the end marker, or the loss masks it out.\n- Good offline, bad online: input distribution, template / sampling differences, context truncation, quantization and batching.","L1-1.16.8":"The key developments in the LLM foundation layer during 2025–2026 can be strung into a timeline. Being able to explain \"what happened and why it matters\" at each milestone in an interview shows you keep up with the field.\n\n**How it works**\n- 2025-01 DeepSeek-R1 open-sourced: an open-weight reasoning model that published a recipe for training reasoning with RL.\n- 2025-04/05 Llama 4, Qwen3: MoE and native multimodality; Qwen3 unified thinking / non-thinking in a single model, making \"hybrid reasoning\" standard.\n- 2025-08 gpt-oss and GPT-5.\n- 2025-09→12 DeepSeek-V3.2 introduces DSA, the first time sparse attention entered a mainstream flagship.\n- 2025-10 Kimi Linear: KDA mixed with MLA, cutting KV cache by up to 75%.\n- 2026-01 DeepSeek Engram; 2026-02 Opus 4.6 replaces `budget_tokens` with adaptive thinking + effort.\n- 2026-03 FlashAttention-4 rewrites its kernels for Blackwell; Mamba-3; GPT-5.4 builds in computer use.\n- 2026-04 DeepSeek-V4 delivers \"affordable 1M context\" with CSA + HCA; Opus 4.7 changes the tokenizer.\n- 2026-09 all three closed vendors update their main models in the same month.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nThere are three main threads: attention and KV cache keep getting compressed (DSA, KDA, CSA); reasoning control moves from \"budget\" to \"intent\" (effort); and long context shifts from \"is it possible\" to \"how cheap is it.\"\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Be able to say \"why it matters\" in one sentence for each milestone.\n- Distinguish \"reportedly\" from \"officially confirmed.\"","L1-1.16.9":"DeepSeek Engram (2026-01, the DeepSeek and Peking University paper \"Conditional Memory via Scalable Lookup\") proposes \"conditional memory\": store static knowledge in hash lookup tables, as a \"sparse memory\" axis complementary to MoE's \"sparse compute.\"\n\n**How it works**\nHash the N-grams in the input and look up the corresponding static memory vectors in a large embedding table; then gate the retrieved vectors into the residual stream, letting the model decide how much of this memory to use in the current context. Lookup cost is almost independent of table size, so a large parameter count can store knowledge without significantly increasing per-token compute. The paper reports that Engram-27B consistently outperforms an MoE baseline at equal parameter count and compute (author-reported).\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nThis is the flip side of the observation that \"knowledge lives in the FFN\": since a large share of FFN capacity goes to memorizing static facts, hand static knowledge to cheap lookups and spend compute on reasoning. It and MoE are two orthogonal sparsification axes: MoE sparsely selects computation, Engram sparsely selects memory. The limitation is that these are still paper results; the repository does not show it being used in any released DeepSeek model.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- One-liner: N-gram hash lookup + gated fusion into the residual stream.\n- Relation to MoE: sparse memory vs. sparse compute, complementary.\n- Don't claim \"V4 uses Engram.\"","L1-1.16.10":"A set of hands-on exercises you can do on a Mac that turn LLM fundamentals into \"I verified this myself\" interview material: implementing a decoder block from scratch, tokenizer cost, KV cache capacity, decoding strategies, template breaking, and reasoning-level and long-context experiments.\n\n**How it works**\n1. **Modern decoder block**: implement RMSNorm, RoPE, GQA with a causal mask, SwiGLU and Pre-LN in PyTorch or MLX. Acceptance criteria: step-by-step logits with and without KV cache satisfy `allclose` (tolerance 1e-4), and causality and RoPE shift-invariance tests pass.\n2. **Tokenizer comparison**: token counts for 6 kinds of text (including Southeast Asian languages, code and JSON) across several tokenizers, estimating cost differences.\n3. **KV cache calculator**: read `config.json` to estimate per-token cache and maximum concurrency, with error within 15% for GQA models and correct handling of sliding-window layers.\n4. **Decoding strategies**: a temperature × top-k / top-p / min-p grid, measuring factual accuracy, diversity and JSON parse rate.\n5. **Chat template breaking**: remove the generation prompt, swap role markers, duplicate BOS, use the wrong stop token; compare accuracy and write a template self-check function.\n6. **Reasoning-level and position experiments**: a quality–cost–latency table from an effort sweep, a position × length heatmap, and cache-hit verification.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nEach exercise has clear acceptance criteria, and the results can be organized into a public repository; exercise 6 uses cloud APIs, so be sure to set a budget cap first.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Be able to cite numbers you measured yourself and counterintuitive findings.\n- The template self-check function can be reused directly in fine-tuning and deployment projects.","L1-1.17.1":"The GPT-6 series is OpenAI's main closed model lineup as of 2026-09, positioned in three tiers: Astra is the strongest, Sol the best value, Luna the high-throughput option, all with 1.05M context and 128K maximum output.\n\n**How it works**\n- GPT-6 Astra (2026-09-03): the top tier, priced at $10 / $50 per million tokens (input / output).\n- GPT-6 Sol / Luna (2026-09-22), GPT-6.1 Sol (2026-09-29): Sol is the value tier at about $2 / $10 (which model this applies to is to be verified); Luna is the high-throughput tier at 0.1 / 0.5.\n- `reasoning.effort` goes up to `max`; Astra and 6.1 Sol both have a knowledge cutoff of 2026-04-30.\n- Evolution: GPT-5.5 (2026-04, 1.05M context) → GPT-5.6 (public on 07-09, the first to use Sol / Terra / Luna tier naming, with several price cuts afterward) → GPT-6.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nThe three-tier design maps onto routing: Astra for hard tasks, Sol for routine tasks, Luna for high-volume simple tasks, with costs differing by two orders of magnitude. GPT-6 Astra sparked discussion about looped depth and reasoning monitorability, but whether it uses a looped transformer is only reported by the media and not confirmed by OpenAI; OpenAI's public statement is that its computation-graph depth is within twice that of GPT-4.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Use the three-tier positioning to explain the cost logic of tiered routing.\n- When discussing Astra's architecture, say \"reportedly.\"\n- Prices change often; note when you checked any numbers you cite.","L1-1.17.2":"Claude Fable 5.1, Opus 5.5 and Sonnet 5.5 are Anthropic's main models as of 2026-09, all with 1M context / 128K output, paired with adaptive thinking and effort levels; Fable is a new tier above Opus.\n\n**How it works**\n- Release dates: Fable 5.1 (2026-09-01), Opus 5.5 (09-22), Sonnet 5.5 (09-28), plus Haiku 4.5 (200K / 64K).\n- Pricing (input / output, USD per million tokens): Fable 5.1 at 10 / 50, Opus 5.5 at 4 / 20, Sonnet 5.5 at 2 / 10, Haiku 4.5 at 1 / 5.\n- Adaptive thinking: the model decides how much to think, always on for Fable and Opus; effort levels express intent, replacing the earlier manual `budget_tokens`.\n- Fable's origin: the 2026-06-09 announcement described Fable 5 as \"a Mythos-class model, safety-hardened and available for general use\"; Mythos is available only to authorized participants in Project Glasswing, and Mythos 5.1 has the same specs as Fable 5.1. Per the Fable 5 announcement, cybersecurity, bio / chem and distillation requests flagged by classifiers are handed to Opus 4.8 instead (fewer than 5% of sessions); whether Fable 5.1 keeps this behavior should be checked in official docs.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nThe reasoning control interface has shifted from \"budget\" to \"intent.\" Starting with Opus 4.7, the tokenizer changed (the same text takes about 1–1.35x as many tokens) and non-default sampling parameters were withdrawn, so cost and configuration need re-evaluation when migrating.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Explain the difference between effort and `budget_tokens`.\n- A tokenizer change is a hidden price increase.\n- The relationship between Mythos and Fable, and the access restrictions.","L1-1.17.3":"Gemini 3.8 Flash and Gemini 3.1 Pro are Google's main closed models as of 2026-09, featuring native multimodal input and thinking level. Notably, the announced Gemini 3.5 Pro had still not launched as of 09-30.\n\n**How it works**\n- Gemini 3.8 Flash (2026-09-02) and Gemini 3.1 Pro (preview, 2026-02-19), both with 1,048,576 context and 65,536 maximum output.\n- Natively accept text, image, video, audio and PDF input; thinking level controls thinking intensity.\n- The Flash line iterates very quickly: 3.5 Flash GA on 05-19 → 3.6 Flash on 07-21 → 3.7 Flash on 08-13 → 3.8 Flash on 09-02.\n- At Gemini 3.1 Pro's launch, media accounts relayed Google's report of a verified ARC-AGI-2 score of 77.1%, more than double that of 3 Pro.\n- Gemini 3.5 Pro: announced at I/O on 2026-05-19 as coming \"next month,\" then delayed repeatedly according to second-hand reports; the highest Pro model listed on the official model page is still 3.1 Pro.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nGemini has the most complete multimodal input, suited to processing video, audio and PDF directly; the Flash line's rapid iteration means production environments need pinned versions and continuous regression evaluation. The thought signatures introduced in Gemini 3 are the same kind of engineering problem as passing back Claude's thinking blocks.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Don't cite Gemini 3.5 Pro as an established fact.\n- The output cap is 65,536, lower than OpenAI's and Anthropic's 128K.\n- When citing the ARC-AGI-2 number, give the source and the context of benchmark saturation.","L1-1.17.4":"Grok 4.7 is xAI's strongest model as of 2026-09; Muse Spark is Meta's closed model series, and its arrival marks Meta's shift from Llama's open-weight approach to closed models.\n\n**How it works**\n- **Grok 4.7** (2026-09-21; date from second-hand reports): 500K context, a rare non-1M-class model among mainstream flagships; official docs call it the strongest current model; priced at $2 / $6 (prompts up to 200K) and $4 / $12 beyond 200K, i.e. tiered pricing by context length. Grok 5 had still not been released as of 09-30 (second-hand reports).\n- **Muse Spark** (first released 2026-04-08): the first model in Meta's new Muse series, developed by Meta Superintelligence Labs, closed, with API access initially limited to a private preview for select partners; versions 1.1 / 1.2 / 1.3 came on 07-09 / 08-05 / 09-02 according to Wikipedia, with about 1M context. Meta officially said it \"hopes to open-source future versions,\" and in August it released the weights of Muse Glimmer 30B under Apache 2.0.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nGrok's tiered pricing is a reminder that the unit price of long prompts can double, so cost estimates should use the real token distribution. Meta's pivot shows that the strategy of \"big open-source players\" isn't stable, and depending on one vendor's open series carries supply risk.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Grok 4.7's 500K is the exception to \"context is no longer a differentiator.\"\n- Meta's trajectory: open Llama → closed Muse Spark → back to open weights with Muse Glimmer.\n- Much of this is second-hand; state your sources when citing it.","L1-1.17.5":"gpt-oss is an Apache 2.0 open-weight MoE reasoning model released by OpenAI in 2025-08; a few days later OpenAI released GPT-5. The key engineering points of gpt-oss are MXFP4 quantization and the mandatory harmony chat format.\n\n**How it works**\n- Specs: gpt-oss-120b has about 117B total / 5.1B active parameters, 36 layers, 128 experts with top-4 routing; gpt-oss-20b is 20.9B / 3.6B. Context 131,072 (extended with YaRN).\n- Attention: banded window of 128 alternating with dense layers, GQA (8 KV heads).\n- MXFP4 quantization: MoE + 4-bit quantization lets the 120b run on a single 80GB GPU.\n- Harmony format: gpt-oss explicitly requires the harmony chat format; otherwise it won't work properly.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nIt is OpenAI's first open-weight model since GPT-2, with a permissive license allowing commercial use and fine-tuning; gpt-oss-20b belongs to the tier you can \"deploy and fine-tune on your own GPUs.\" It is also the best example of why the chat template matters: with the wrong template, performance degrades badly, which is exactly the first thing to check when an open model underperforms.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Explain why a 100B-class model can run on a single GPU: few active parameters + 4-bit weights.\n- The lesson of the harmony format: verify the template and stop tokens before deploying.\n- Be able to contrast gpt-oss and GPT-5: the former is open-weight and self-hostable; the latter is a closed API flagship.","L1-1.17.6":"Qwen3.5 and Qwen3.8 are Alibaba's open-weight flagship models, representing the arrival of \"hybrid linear attention + MoE\" in the open-source mainstream: Gated DeltaNet and Gated Attention mixed at 3:1.\n\n**How it works**\n- **Qwen3.5** (2026-02): 397B / 17B active plus several smaller sizes; the layer structure is 15 × (3 × DeltaNet + 1 × Attention); early-fusion multimodality, merging the previously separate VL branch into the main line; Apache 2.0.\n- **Qwen3.8** (2026-08): 2.4T-A95B (weights on 08-12) is 23 × the same 4-layer group, 92 layers in total, text-only input, custom license; the 27B dense model (08-14) natively supports images and video, Apache 2.0.\n- Native context of 262,144, extendable to about 1.01M (Alibaba Cloud's API lists 1M).\n- Reasoning control: `reasoning_effort` (xhigh / medium / low) and `preserve_thinking`.\n- Evolution: Qwen3-Next (2025-09) → Qwen3.5 → Qwen3.8.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nThe 3:1 mix uses Gated DeltaNet for cheap context aggregation and a few global attention layers for precise retrieval, greatly reducing long-context KV cache. Different sizes in the same series differ in modality support and license: the 27B is the workhorse for self-hosting and fine-tuning, while the 2.4T can only be used on clusters or via hosted services.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Write out the 3:1 layer layout and explain the motivation.\n- Stress checking modality and license per specific model.","L1-1.17.7":"Muse Glimmer 30B is a ~30B dense model whose weights Meta released on 2026-08-10 under Apache 2.0 (per Wikipedia and media reports). It emphasizes extremely low KV cache usage and represents an extreme GQA configuration.\n\n**How it works**\n- GQA: 32 query heads share just 2 KV heads (Llama 3 8B is 32 : 8); the fewer the KV heads, the less K and V cached per token.\n- Local / global 3:1: 3 of every 4 layers use sliding-window attention, caching only tokens within the window.\n- Gated attention and Gemma-style RMSNorm before and after sublayers. 131K context.\n- Effect: among ~30B models, Muse Glimmer 30B uses about 52 KiB / token, while Gemma 4 31B uses about 840 KiB / token (BF16, per the blog's conversion method) — a difference of more than an order of magnitude.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nKV cache determines memory usage and maximum concurrency at long context; Glimmer's design lets the same memory serve longer contexts or more concurrent requests, suiting self-hosting. The cost is that so few KV heads may lose some expressiveness, which training has to compensate for. It also marks Meta's return to open weights after the closed Muse Spark, and sits in the ~30B \"self-hostable and fine-tunable\" battleground.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Be able to explain why the cache is so small using KV head count and the proportion of local layers.\n- When comparing \"cache per token,\" first confirm the counting method (whether local layers are included, precision).\n- Be able to compute by hand the cache ratio of GQA relative to MHA.","L1-1.17.8":"Mistral's current lineup includes Large 3, Medium 3.5 and Small 4. Large 3 is Mistral's first sparse MoE since Mixtral; Small 4 combines instruct, reasoning and coding in a single model.\n\n**How it works**\n- **Mistral Large 3** (2025-12-02): 675B total / 41B active parameters (official figure; Raschka's gallery says 673B), sparse MoE, Apache 2.0. It is one of the milestones where an Apache 2.0 ultra-large MoE appeared on the open side.\n- **Mistral Medium 3.5** (2026-04): 128B dense (second-hand summary, to be verified), modified MIT license.\n- **Mistral Small 4** (2026-03): 119B / 6.5B active (second-hand summary, to be verified), combining instruct, reasoning and coding abilities, Apache 2.0.\n- Medium 3.5 and Small 4 reportedly have 256K context, per second-hand sources.\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nMistral is Europe's main open-weight vendor, with mostly Apache 2.0 licenses, making it an option for scenarios with data residency and compliance requirements. Small 4's \"three-in-one\" approach matches Qwen3's hybrid reasoning idea: fewer models to maintain. Some specs are second-hand and should be flagged as such when cited.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Large 3's significance: an Apache 2.0 ultra-large MoE.\n- License differences: Large 3 and Small 4 are Apache 2.0, Medium 3.5 is modified MIT.\n- How \"three-in-one\" models simplify deployment and routing.","L1-1.17.9":"Llama 4 (2025-04-05) is the generation of open-weight models in which Meta moved from dense to MoE and adopted early-fusion native multimodality; Scout's iRoPE is the representative example of the \"some layers without positional encoding\" idea.\n\n**How it works**\n- Specs: Scout has 109B total / 17B active parameters (16 experts) and 10M context (official); Maverick is 400B / 17B (128 experts), with 1M context according to second-hand sources.\n- Early fusion: image and text tokens are trained jointly in the same sequence from pre-training onward, rather than via a bolted-on vision encoder.\n- iRoPE: interleaved attention layers use no positional encoding (NoPE), and attention is temperature-scaled at inference to improve length generalization. The rationale is that a causal model can infer position from the mask itself, and adjacent RoPE layers also provide order information.\n- License: Llama 4 Community License (second-hand source).\n\n**Why it matters / trade-offs**\nLlama 4 marked \"dense → MoE\" becoming the general migration direction for large models, and native multimodality entering the open-source mainstream. iRoPE supports extremely long context, though pure NoPE still generally underperforms RoPE at scale. Meta later moved to the closed Muse Spark, then returned to open weights with Muse Glimmer.\n\n**Interview notes**\n- Explain iRoPE: some layers drop positional encoding + attention temperature scaling at inference.\n- Why it works without positional encoding: the causal mask implies position.\n- The Community License must be read clause by clause; it is not equivalent to Apache 2.0."});
